window.__CS249R_CHAPTERS__ = window.__CS249R_CHAPTERS__ || {};
window.__CS249R_CHAPTERS__["vol4/18_frontier"] = {"key": "vol4/18_frontier", "sections": [{"id": "sec-1", "level": 2, "title": "Purpose"}, {"id": "sec-2", "level": 2, "title": "From a Better Prediction to Useful Service"}, {"id": "sec-3", "level": 3, "title": "What the release claim rests on"}, {"id": "sec-4", "level": 3, "title": "Conditional shield credit and common-cause bounds"}, {"id": "sec-5", "level": 2, "title": "Choosing the Next Capability"}, {"id": "sec-6", "level": 3, "title": "Match the experiment to the missing evidence"}, {"id": "sec-7", "level": 3, "title": "What a restriction costs"}], "html": "<h1 id=\"sec-1\" class=\"cs-h1\">Engineering Physical AI</h1>\n<h2 id=\"sec-1\" class=\"cs-h2\">Purpose</h2>\n<p><em>Why does a better model not, by itself, make a better machine?</em></p>\n<p>The learned model is the part of a physical machine that is easiest to improve and easiest to score, so it attracts most of the effort. Useful service depends on everything around it as well. The body must sustain the task across loads, surfaces, and hours of duty. The learned behavior must hold in the states its own actions create. Each proposal must be admitted, corrected, or refused before the motion it asks for is already under way. The evidence for all of it must match the conditions of operation and stay true as the machine and its site change. Each of these can be engineered well in isolation while the machine as a whole remains unjustified, because doubt accumulates across every premise a physical claim depends on, and a premise that no one names or owns is assumed silently. A team that cannot join these parts into one argument keeps improving the model while an unmeasured floor, an unqualified detector, or an unowned restriction decides what the machine actually does. The same joined argument also chooses what to build next, since the most valuable measurement, experience, or design change is the one that retires the weakest open premise. At the causal boundary, irreversibility makes that weakest premise, not the best prediction, the measure of what a machine may be trusted to do.</p>\n<p>{{&lt; pagebreak &gt;}}</p>\n<div class=\"cs-callout\"><div class=\"cs-callout-title\">Learning Objectives</div>\n<ul class=\"cs-list\">\n<li>Explain why a better prediction can still produce a worse physical action, and synthesize the four laws into the design argument that answers it</li>\n<li>Distinguish an unobserved premise from a monitored unknown, and explain why only a standing restriction can contain an unobserved premise</li>\n<li>Apply the four laws to select and evaluate a candidate operating mode for an unfamiliar machine</li>\n<li>Construct residual-claims entries with supported restrictions, accountable owners, and predeclared closure tests</li>\n<li>Evaluate the throughput cost of an operating restriction against the cost of the closure campaign that would retire it</li>\n<li>Select the next measurement, learning experiment, or design change needed to extend a machine's capability</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n<h2 id=\"sec-2\" class=\"cs-h2\">From a Better Prediction to Useful Service</h2>\n<p>On this book's own release and approval records (@sec-release and @sec-responsibility), the warehouse mobile manipulator is refused. Even the stipulated evidence branch earns it only conditional service, held in place by premises that no sensor on the machine reports, such as the friction of the aisle floor. Improving its learned models alone would not change that verdict while the supporting physical and timing premises remain unqualified. That refusal is the engineering form of the answer to the question @sec-boundary opened with: why a better prediction can still produce a worse physical action, and what the rest of the machine must supply so that a model's proposals become useful work within justified limits.</p>\n<p>The question began with a contrast. Traditional machine learning evaluated advisory predictions against static datasets, and agentic machine learning could run iterative reasoning loops inside controlled digital sandboxes, behind glass, with elastic time, transactional rollback, and symbolic interfaces. Those privileges depended on the sandbox; external calls could already produce irreversible effects. Physical AI stepped through that glass into the continuous world, where computation commands actuators across the causal boundary. That step exposed computation to continuous time, in which delay becomes distance traveled, and to mechanical and thermodynamic irreversibility, which forbids undo. The illustrated feedback workload also required one action at a time under a real-time deadline, a batch size of one ($\\BatchSize=1$). This scheduling constraint does not prohibit coordinated axes or batching elsewhere in the system. These constraints forced an architectural response, the separation of speculative proposals from deterministic permission, which is a design choice rather than a fact of physics.</p>\n<p>One reference platform carried the argument: a warehouse mobile manipulator that picks an object from a moving conveyor, opens a spring-latched cage door, and carries the payload down an aisle shared with people. That mission exercised every capability the book develops (sizing the physical and computational dynamics, teaching learned policies, integrating a real-time runtime, evaluating closed-loop behavior, and maintaining the operating envelope as the machine and its site degrade). Each design choice constrained the next.</p>\n<p>Predictive accuracy alone does not establish the operating premises on which physical success depends. What an actuator command does once it crosses the causal boundary depends on the distance a mobile base travels while a neural network runs its forward pass, the friction available at the tire-floor interface during hard braking, the states into which the robot's own prior actions drive it, and the gaps between demonstration datasets and live site conditions. Improving a model's statistical accuracy can refine the proposed trajectory, and shortening inference can recover timing margin, but neither result by itself qualifies traction, closed-loop competence, or the complete stopping budget.</p>\n<p>The second half of the question, what the surrounding machine must supply, resolves into the four-step argument of @sec-boundary-central-question, pairing one engineering discipline with each of the four laws. Under the first law (<em>irreversibility</em>), the body's mechanical and thermal limits bound what any proposal may request. Part I translated payload capacity, surface traction, contact forces, motor heating, and energy reserves into strict operating budgets that govern proposal horizons, execution cadence, and deterministic fallback paths. Under the second law (<em>endogenous data</em>), policy competence holds only over the states induced by closed-loop interaction. Part II tied proposer evaluation directly to the states the physical machine actually visits, turning recovery failures into targeted data-collection runs rather than unhandled anomalies. Under the third law (<em>proposal is not permission</em>), execution authority resides in an independent permission path rather than the learned model. Part III joined observation freshness contracts, grounded geometric targets, timed trajectories, and real-time admission filters onto a compute layout built to meet hard deadlines under load. Under the fourth law (<em>permission must be earned</em>), deployment scope extends only as far as qualified system-wide evidence supports it. Part IV structured physical operation as a governed service contract that separates proposer autonomy, whole-system task success, and protective intervention. A least-change speed filter, for example, can preserve an in-flight delivery even when a raw proposal requires intervention, whereas an emergency stop protects physical hardware without fulfilling the promised service.</p>\n<p>The release verdict is where the four laws meet. In the baseline audit of @sec-release-case-worked-full, the book's illustrative records yield <code>REFUSE</code>, because tail risks and fault margins remain unverified. The stipulated branch of @sec-release-stipulated-service shows which evidence would raise that verdict to conditional authorization (<code>COND</code>). Under those stipulated performance and fault records, the machine may run inspected-route transport, guarded door approaches, and close-proximity handovers, provided that standing conditions on floor traction, pedestrian crossings, feeder alignment, and sensor calibration stay verified. Each condition traces back through the record chain to a measured limit, an evaluated behavior, or a timed check, which is what separates a result stated under premises from a deployment claim.</p>\n<pre class=\"cs-code\"><code>#| echo: false\n# ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n# │ CLOSED-LOOP SYNTHESIS FIGURE LABELS\n# ├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n# │ Context: @fig-18-causal-loop-synthesis (SVG labels are hand-typed).\n# │ Goal:    Keep the machine&#x27;s rates and bus voltage drawn in the figure\n# │          equal to the registry values the rest of the book prints.\n# │ Show:    Nothing; the cell only guards the drawn labels.\n# │ How:     Compare each drawn label with the registered machine value.\n# └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nfrom mlsysim import *\nfrom mlsysim.core.units import Hz, kHz, ms, volt\n\nRM = Embodied.MobileManipulator.WarehouseMobileManipulator\n\nclass LoopSynthesisFigure:\n    &quot;&quot;&quot;Values drawn in fig18_closed_loop_synthesis.svg.&quot;&quot;&quot;\n\n    # ┌── 1. LOAD (Drawn labels and the registry values they must match) ──────\n    drawn = {\n        &quot;intent rate (Hz)&quot;: (2.0, RM.intent_rate.to(Hz).magnitude),\n        &quot;intent inference (ms)&quot;: (336.0, RM.intent_reference_latency.to(ms).magnitude),\n        &quot;chunk renewal (ms)&quot;: (50.0, (1 / RM.chunk_rate).to(ms).magnitude),\n        &quot;setpoint step (ms)&quot;: (25.0, RM.chunk_step.to(ms).magnitude),\n        &quot;permission tick (kHz)&quot;: (1.0, RM.permission_rate.to(kHz).magnitude),\n        &quot;drive bus nominal (V)&quot;: (47.7, RM.drive_bus_nominal.to(volt).magnitude),\n        &quot;drive current loop (kHz)&quot;: (20.0, RM.current_loop_rate.to(kHz).magnitude),\n    }\n\n    # ┌── 3. GUARD (Invariants) ────────────────────────────────────────────────\n    for _name, (_label, _value) in drawn.items():\n        check(abs(_label - _value) &lt; 1e-9, f&quot;closed-loop figure label for {_name} is stale ({_label} vs {_value})&quot;)\n\n    # ┌── 4. OUTPUT (Formatting) ───────────────────────────────────────────────\n    intent_rate_str = fmt_frequency(RM.intent_rate, unit=Hz, precision=0)\n    renewal_str = fmt_latency(1 / RM.chunk_rate, unit=ms, precision=0)\n    setpoint_str = fmt_latency(RM.chunk_step, unit=ms, precision=0)\n    permission_str = fmt_frequency(RM.permission_rate, unit=kHz, precision=0)\n    bus_str = fmt_voltage(RM.drive_bus_nominal, precision=1)\n    current_loop_str = fmt_frequency(RM.current_loop_rate, unit=kHz, precision=0)\n]```\n\nAssembled, these parts close one causal loop through the physical world (@fig-18-causal-loop-synthesis). The loop runs at rates four orders of magnitude apart: the Brain proposes at `[LoopSynthesisFigure.intent_rate_str]`, the permission path on the discrete safety microcontroller admits, corrects, or refuses at `[LoopSynthesisFigure.permission_str]`, and the drives close their current loops at `[LoopSynthesisFigure.current_loop_str]`. The loop closes only through the World, and the World returns only what its sensors capture, with capture times, to the permission path and to the Brain. A state the sensors never carry, such as the friction under a clear film, is invisible to every level at once. Governance surrounds all five levels and changes on the timescale of releases, so it sets the budgets and standing conditions that the fast loop enforces without taking part in any single control cycle.\n\n::: {#fig-18-causal-loop-synthesis fig-env=&quot;figure&quot; fig-pos=&quot;t&quot; fig-cap=&quot;**Closed causal loop**: The Brain proposes across the proposal boundary, and the permission path in the Nervous System admits, modifies, or refuses each proposal before the Body acts. The Body&#x27;s current becomes force and motion at the Boundary, the World evolves, and the Boundary returns measurements with capture times to the permission path and the Brain. Not every state of the World reaches a sensor. The governance envelope surrounds all five levels and changes on the timescale of releases rather than control cycles. The labels are this machine&#x27;s values: the intent model proposes at `[LoopSynthesisFigure.intent_rate_str]`, the chunk is renewed every `[LoopSynthesisFigure.renewal_str]`, the application processor sends a setpoint every `[LoopSynthesisFigure.setpoint_str]` over a black-channel link to a discrete safety microcontroller unit (MCU) whose permission path ticks at `[LoopSynthesisFigure.permission_str]`, and the drives close their current loops at `[LoopSynthesisFigure.current_loop_str]` from a `[LoopSynthesisFigure.bus_str]` bus. Another machine sets its own rates.&quot; fig-alt=&quot;Five stacked levels inside a dashed governance envelope: Brain, Nervous System, Body, Boundary, and World, with a dashed proposal boundary between the Brain and the Nervous System. Inside the Nervous System an application processor sends setpoints over a black-channel link to a discrete safety MCU that runs the permission path. Arrows run from the Brain to the application processor, labeled proposal, from the safety MCU to the Body, labeled admitted command, and on to the World, labeled current in a winding and force and motion. A return arrow runs from the World to the Boundary, then up a side lane to the safety MCU, labeled fresh state, and to the Brain, labeled observations. Labels give the intent model 2 Hz with 336 ms inference, chunk renewal every 50 ms, a setpoint every 25 ms, a 1 kHz permission tick, a 20 kHz current loop in the drives, and a 47.7 V drive bus; the Boundary is event-timed, the World is continuous, and governance has no control rate.&quot;}\n\n:::\n\nThe records summarized in @tbl-18-master-synthesis bring the four laws into one engineering workflow. Rather than treating safety as an abstract property of a model, each part of the architecture leaves behind durable, auditable records that preserve the evidence and operating intent when tasks, payloads, or site conditions change.\n\n\\Needspace{25\\baselineskip}\n\n| Law                            | Part                                                                                             | Records that carry it                                                                                               | Condition to maintain                                                                                                                                                              | Engineering result                                                                                                             |\n|:-------------------------------|:-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|:--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|:-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|:-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| **Irreversibility**            | I (@sec-body, @sec-brain, @sec-nervous)                                                          | Limit records; the proposer&#x27;s limits, which enter the handoff record                                                | Each measured limit, such as floor friction, payload, brake onset, or observation age, still holds under present conditions                                                        | A feasible speed or force envelope, a timed proposal interface, and a reachable fallback                                       |\n| **Endogenous data**            | II (@sec-data, @sec-training, @sec-evaluation)                                                   | Provenance record, policy manifest, evaluation record                                                               | The states the machine enters lie inside the target operational design domain (ODD, @sec-data-curse-dimensionality-recoverability-geometry) that closed-loop evaluation represents | A learned behavior evaluated in its closed loop; new qualified experience can expand its supported task population             |\n| **Proposal is not permission** | III (@sec-perception, @sec-memory, @sec-intent, @sec-planning, @sec-enforcement, @sec-placement) | Observation contract, belief record, intent lease, trajectory record, enforcement record, placement record          | The permission path sees fresh, distinguishable state and meets its deadline under load                                                                                            | Useful motion admitted or minimally corrected within current limits; invalid active authority invokes a state-matched fallback |\n| **Permission must be earned**  | IV (@sec-intervention, @sec-verification, @sec-release, @sec-responsibility)                     | Authority record, fault record, release manifest, approval record, and the residual-claims register of this chapter | The premises of the release claim still hold after release                                                                                                                         | A named service authorized under standing conditions, with tested intervention and update procedures                           |\n\n: **The four laws as engineering competence**: Each part produces records that support the next design decision. The conditions and results belong to the particular machine and its evidence; they are not universal hardware requirements. {#tbl-18-master-synthesis tbl-colwidths=&quot;[14,17,25,22,22]&quot;}\n\nThe synthesis also completes the history traced in @sec-boundary-prediction-to-action. Over eight decades, learning moved from the machine&#x27;s periphery toward its actuators. Deterministic mechanisms gave way to learned perception feeding analytical planners, then to end-to-end motor policies, and now to foundation models that propose action chunks across many robot bodies. At each step, a guarantee that physical separation or a watchful person once supplied had to move inside the machine.\n\nThe warehouse platform shows where that history now stands. Its split between an intent model proposing at `[LoopSynthesisFigure.intent_rate_str]` and a chunk policy renewed every `[LoopSynthesisFigure.renewal_str]` mirrors current vision-language-action pipelines, and the guarantees those statistical models displaced survive in the deterministic architecture around them. The reflex halt beneath Shakey&#x27;s STRIPS planner and the lower layers of Brooks&#x27;s subsumption architecture, which kept running while higher layers were busy or wrong, have become the discrete permission path, enforcing signed kinematic and dynamic envelopes sized for this body. The remote operators who stayed close to the loop at the DARPA Robotics Challenge have become a formal intervention authority with timed handover budgets, and the environmental oversight a person once supplied has become explicit standing conditions of release.\n\nThe next phase, taken up in @sec-frontier-four-open-challenges, brings foundation models that control many bodies while learning continuously from fleet deployments. Cross-embodiment pretraining broadens what a model can propose, and it also carries the model further from the empirical distribution of any single site. Wider proposing latitude therefore makes the independent permission path more necessary, not less. It demands stronger checks at runtime and more defensible evidence at release, an architectural response to the safety-limit and conformity requirements of European machinery law (@sec-release-standards-map).\n\nThe architecture is a method meant to carry across platforms. Its transferable core consists of the foundational questions, dynamic budgets, test protocols, and interface boundaries; the platform-specific elements are the calibrated numerical thresholds, kinematic envelopes, and actuator limits. The rest of this chapter tests how that core transfers to an unfamiliar packaging cell (@sec-frontier-generalizing-unfamiliar-systems), tracks the premises no runtime sensor can check in a residual-claims register (@sec-frontier-epistemic-limits), and uses those open premises to select the next capability to build (@sec-frontier-assumptions-change).\n\n## Generalizing to Unfamiliar Systems {#sec-frontier-generalizing-unfamiliar-systems}\n\nAn automated packaging cell that shuttles containers, seats closure tabs, and repeats high-rate cycles across a full shift can ram a closing gate, crush a carton wall, or bake its motor windings if its learned proposers outrun its physical limits. Building it requires the same pairing as the warehouse machine: a capable learned proposer with an execution pipeline whose runtime authority respects kinematic reach, contact force, and thermal buildup in the drive motors. The four bedrock laws trace one chain through such a machine. The chain runs from the task&#x27;s irreversible consequences to measurable physical envelopes, through the closed-loop data that trains and validates its policies, to the real-time runtime that gates motor commands, and finally to the operating envelope certified for deployment. Auditing a machine the book has not yet studied tests whether that chain is a transferable method or only a description of one robot.\n\n::: {.column-margin .margin-pointer}\n↩ The four bedrock laws, from irreversibility to permission must be earned, are established in @sec-boundary-bedrock-laws.\n:::\n\n### Auditing a new machine with the four laws {#sec-frontier-four-law-audit-protocol}\n\nAuditing an unfamiliar machine out of order (evaluating a learned policy before the stopping budget fixes the speed ceiling, for example) wastes the evaluation on states the permission path will never admit. The audit therefore asks one question per law, in the order the laws constrain one another, and each answer leaves a record behind:\n\n1. **Irreversibility.** What can the task do that no later command can undo, and what measured bound keeps it from happening? The answer names the collisions, plastic deformations, and thermal overloads of the task and sizes the envelope that excludes them, such as a speed ceiling from the stopping budget. It produces the machine&#x27;s limit records.\n2. **Endogenous data.** Do the demonstrations and the closed-loop evaluation cover the states the machine will enter once that envelope is enforced? A limit added in step 1 changes the states the proposer sees, so the evaluation must be run inside the envelope, not before it. It produces the provenance record and the evaluation record.\n3. **Proposal is not permission.** Which independent path admits, corrects, or refuses each proposal, on which sensors, within which timing budget, and with which fallback when permission lapses? It produces the enforcement record and the timing budget the permission path must meet.\n4. **Permission must be earned.** Which operating claims does the assembled evidence support, under which standing conditions, who answers for each decision behind them, and which premises remain open? It produces the release manifest, the approval record, and the residual-claims register entries for every premise left open.\n\nBecause each step constrains the next, adjustments ripple back up the list. Reducing the permitted transit speed to satisfy a stopping budget in step 1 alters the visual flow and dynamic states that step 2 must evaluate. Tracing those four steps on a high-rate packaging cell shows how a single limit in step 1 reshapes the data, runtime checks, and release claims that follow.\n\n### A packaging cell across the three machine classes {#sec-frontier-packaging-cell-study}\n\nConsider a packaging cell in a logistics hub, laid out as in @fig-18-packaging-cell (panel a). A carriage carrying one carton runs along a straight track, driven by a linear servo drive at the track&#x27;s near end. Partway along, an insertion head presses down on the carton&#x27;s flaps to seat its retention tabs. At the far end, a gate closes across the track whenever the next station cannot accept a carton, and a carriage still moving when the gate closes drives its carton into the gate and the frame. The cell is laid out tightly for cycle rate, so the carriage is only a few centimeters from its stop line when a closure can begin. This single cell couples all three machine classes within one enclosure: the carriage as moving mass, the insertion head as contact force, and the drive as thermal dissipation, because it cycles the carriage through rapid accelerations all shift.\n\n::: {.column-margin .margin-pointer}\n↩ The three machine classes (mass-dominated mobility, contact-dominated manipulation, and energy- and thermal-dominated operation) are established in @sec-boundary-three-classes.\n:::\n\nThe first law asks what the machine cannot undo, so the audit begins by cataloging the cell&#x27;s irreversible state changes alongside the governing physical bounds. A loaded parcel container moving along a linear transfer rail carries momentum. When a carriage strikes an obstruction, kinetic energy dissipates through plastic deformation of the container and structural deflection of the frame, and commanding the drive motor in reverse cannot undo that damage. The insertion head introduces a second irreversible boundary: an actuator drives a tool head against package tabs to lock retaining clips. If the actuator pushes harder than the carton wall can support in compression, the container buckles and crushes its contents. The high-duty drive introduces a third irreversible boundary through Joule heating ($\\WindingLossPower = \\TorqueProducingCurrent^2 \\ElectricalResistance(\\WindingTemperature)$). Continuous high-acceleration cycles pump heat into sealed motor windings. Winding insulation does not need to melt to fail; elevated temperatures speed up chemical degradation over time, shortening insulation life rapidly [@iec60085-2007]. Cooling the housing afterward cannot restore the dielectric strength already lost. Because insulation damage accumulates across duty cycles, an instantaneous temperature trip threshold alone cannot bound lifetime wear.\n</code></pre>\n<p>#| echo: false #| warning: false #| message: false</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ PACKAGING CELL: A CANDIDATE TRANSPORT MODE</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h1\">├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Context: @sec-frontier-packaging-cell-study.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Goal:    Test nominal speed against a fixed stop line, solve the speed</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          ceiling the stop budget admits, and check a restricted speed below it.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Show:    Delay, stopping distances, the ceiling, reserve, and translational energy.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ How:     Hypothetical scenario inputs (Scenarios.PackagingCell*); MLSysIM's</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          elementary stopping budget (StoppingBudgetModel), its positive root,</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          and kinetic energy. These are not measurements.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h1\">└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>from mlsysim import * from mlsysim.core.units import dimensionless, kHz, kg, meter, mm, ms, second</p>\n<p>class PackagingCell:</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 1. LOAD (Scenario assumptions, Scenarios.PackagingCell*) ─────────────</h1>\n<p>_NOM = Scenarios.PackagingCellTransit  # nominal speed, chain, brake, clearance _MODE = Scenarios.PackagingCellRestrictedMode  # the candidate restricted speed _FAST = Scenarios.PackagingCellHalfProcessing  # processing allocation halved _HALF = Scenarios.PackagingCellHalfBrake  # braking bound halved _CHECK = Scenarios.PackagingCellQualifiedBrake  # a lower qualified braking bound (checkpoint estimate) mass = _NOM.mass  # scenario assumption: total moving mass, including load v_nom = _NOM.speed  # scenario assumption: nominal speed v_clamp = _MODE.speed  # scenario assumption: restricted speed clearance = _NOM.clearance  # scenario assumption: clearance to a fixed stop line reserve = _NOM.reserve  # scenario assumption: allocated uncertainty and clearance reserve deceleration = _NOM.deceleration  # scenario assumption: braking lower bound after delay exposure = _NOM.exposure  # scenario assumption: worst-case event-to-usable-frame interval processing = _NOM.processing  # scenario assumption: transfer, inference, scheduling, and admission bus = _NOM.bus  # scenario assumption: admitted command to brake-driver delivery onset = _NOM.onset  # scenario assumption: delivery to full assumed deceleration check_rate = _NOM.check_rate  # scenario assumption: independent kinematic-check rate force_cap = _NOM.force_limit  # scenario assumption: selected limiter setting, not material strength temperature_trip = _NOM.temperature_trip  # scenario assumption: selected sensor threshold stall_timeout = _NOM.stall_timeout  # scenario assumption: selected stall-detection timeout bus_trip = _NOM.bus_trip  # scenario assumption: selected inverter overvoltage threshold deceleration_check = _CHECK.deceleration  # scenario assumption: a lower qualified braking bound (checkpoint estimate)</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 2. EXECUTE (Compute) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>nom, mode = _NOM.result, _MODE.result delay = _NOM.delay stop_budget = _NOM.stop_budget blind_nom = nom.delay_travel brake_nom = nom.braking_travel stop_nom = nom.stop_distance deficit = _NOM.deficit</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h1\">Speed ceiling: positive root of v tau + v^2/(2a) = clearance - reserve</h1>\n<p>v_ceiling = nom.speed_ceiling.to(meter / second)</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h1\">What changes if: halve the processing allocation, or halve the braking bound</h1>\n<p>v_ceiling_fast_proc = _FAST.result.speed_ceiling.to(meter / second) v_ceiling_half_decel = _HALF.result.speed_ceiling.to(meter / second) blind_clamp = mode.delay_travel brake_clamp = mode.braking_travel stop_clamp = mode.stop_distance remaining = _MODE.remaining energy_nom = _NOM.kinetic_energy energy_clamp = _MODE.kinetic_energy energy_reduction = (1 - energy_clamp / energy_nom).to(dimensionless).magnitude</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h1\">Operands of the positive root, shown in the notebook</h1>\n<p>a_tau, two_a_d, root_term = _NOM.root_terms v_ceiling_check = _CHECK.result.speed_ceiling.to(meter / second)</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 3. GUARD (Invariants) ────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h1\">2026-09-28: pin the complete hypothetical budget and the claimed reserve.</h1>\n<p>check(delay == 64 * ms, \"All four delay allocations must total 64 ms.\") check(blind_nom &gt; clearance, \"Nominal speed must exhaust clearance before braking.\") check(abs(stop_nom.to(mm).magnitude - 171) &lt; 1e-9, \"Nominal stop must be 171 mm.\") check(abs(stop_clamp.to(mm).magnitude - 40.3333333333) &lt; 1e-6, \"Restricted stop must be 40.3 mm.\") check(stop_clamp &lt; stop_budget and remaining &gt; reserve, \"Restricted stop must preserve the allocated reserve.\") check(abs(v_ceiling.magnitude - 0.5471) &lt; 5e-4, \"Carriage speed ceiling must be 0.55 m/s.\") check( abs(calc_safe_stopping_distance(v_ceiling, delay, deceleration).to(mm).magnitude - stop_budget.to(mm).magnitude) &lt; 1e-6, \"At the ceiling the stop must fill the stop budget exactly.\", ) check(v_clamp &lt; v_ceiling &lt; v_nom, \"The restricted speed is chosen below the ceiling, which sits below nominal.\") check(abs(v_ceiling_fast_proc.magnitude - 0.6613) &lt; 5e-4, \"Halving processing raises the ceiling to 0.66 m/s.\") check(abs(v_ceiling_half_decel.magnitude - 0.4715) &lt; 5e-4, \"Halving the braking bound lowers the ceiling to 0.47 m/s.\") check(v_ceiling_half_decel &lt; v_clamp, \"At half the braking bound the 0.5 m/s mode no longer fits.\") check(abs(energy_nom.to(joule).magnitude - 13.5) &lt; 1e-9, \"Nominal translational energy must be 13.5 J.\") check(abs(energy_clamp.to(joule).magnitude - 1.5) &lt; 1e-9, \"Restricted translational energy must be 1.5 J.\") check(abs(a_tau.magnitude - 0.96) &lt; 1e-9 and abs(two_a_d.magnitude - 1.35) &lt; 1e-9, \"Root operands must be 0.96 m/s and 1.35 m²/s².\") check(abs((root_term - a_tau - v_ceiling).magnitude) &lt; 1e-9, \"The shown root minus a*tau must equal the ceiling.\") check( abs(v_ceiling_check.magnitude - 0.5242) &lt; 5e-4 and v_ceiling_check &gt; v_clamp, \"At 12 m/s² the ceiling (0.52 m/s) must still admit the 0.5 m/s mode.\", )</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 4. OUTPUT (Formatting) ───────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>mass_str = fmt_qty(mass, unit=kg, precision=0) v_nom_str = fmt_velocity(v_nom, precision=2) v_clamp_str = fmt_velocity(v_clamp, precision=2) v_ceiling_str = fmt_velocity(v_ceiling, precision=2) v_ceiling_fast_proc_str = fmt_velocity(v_ceiling_fast_proc, precision=2) v_ceiling_half_decel_str = fmt_velocity(v_ceiling_half_decel, precision=2) processing_half_str = fmt_latency(processing / 2, unit=ms, precision=0) deceleration_half_str = fmt_acceleration(deceleration / 2, precision=1) clearance_str = fmt_length(clearance, unit=mm, precision=0) reserve_str = fmt_length(reserve, unit=mm, precision=0) deceleration_str = fmt_acceleration(deceleration, precision=0) exposure_str = fmt_latency(exposure, unit=ms, precision=0) processing_str = fmt_latency(processing, unit=ms, precision=0) bus_str = fmt_latency(bus, unit=ms, precision=0) onset_str = fmt_latency(onset, unit=ms, precision=0) delay_str = fmt_latency(delay, unit=ms, precision=0) blind_nom_str = fmt_length(blind_nom, unit=mm, precision=0) brake_nom_str = fmt_length(brake_nom, unit=mm, precision=0) stop_nom_str = fmt_length(stop_nom, unit=mm, precision=0) deficit_str = fmt_length(deficit, unit=mm, precision=0) stop_budget_str = fmt_length(stop_budget, unit=mm, precision=0) blind_clamp_str = fmt_length(blind_clamp, unit=mm, precision=0) brake_clamp_str = fmt_length(brake_clamp, unit=mm, precision=1) stop_clamp_str = fmt_length(stop_clamp, unit=mm, precision=1) remaining_str = fmt_length(remaining, unit=mm, precision=1) energy_nom_str = fmt_energy(energy_nom, unit=joule, precision=1) energy_clamp_str = fmt_energy(energy_clamp, unit=joule, precision=1) energy_reduction_str = fmt_percent(energy_reduction, precision=1, style=\"prose\") check_rate_str = fmt_frequency(check_rate, unit=kHz, precision=0) force_cap_str = fmt_force(force_cap, precision=0) temperature_trip_str = fmt_temperature(temperature_trip, precision=0) stall_timeout_str = fmt_latency(stall_timeout, unit=ms, precision=0) bus_trip_str = fmt_voltage(bus_trip, precision=0) a_tau_str = fmt_velocity(a_tau, precision=2) two_a_d_str = fmt_qty(two_a_d, unit=meter<strong>2 / second</strong>2, precision=2) root_term_str = fmt_velocity(root_term, precision=2) deceleration_check_str = fmt_acceleration(deceleration_check, precision=0) ]```</p>\n<p>Because the carriage's momentum is already committed, the permission path must act before the carriage consumes its clearance. The stop modeled next is the cell's application-level path: a camera watches the gate, a learned model recognizes that a closure has begun, and the admitted stop command goes to the carriage brake. That path keeps normal operation clear of the gate, but it is not the cell's certified protective function, which the upcoming third-law discussion and Problem 1 take up. The constant-deceleration stopping relation of @sec-body-stopping-distance-prebrake tests a candidate design for it, here on hypothetical scenario inputs rather than measurements from a commissioned cell.</p>\n<p><strong>Problem</strong>: The loaded carriage travels at <code>[PackagingCell.v_nom_str]</code> toward a gate whose fixed stop line is <code>[PackagingCell.clearance_str]</code> away when the hazardous closure begins, maintaining constant speed until braking begins. Can it stop short of the line, and if not, what is the fastest speed whose stop fits once a <code>[PackagingCell.reserve_str]</code> reserve is held back?</p>\n<p><strong>Variables</strong>:</p>\n<ul class=\"cs-list\">\n<li>Moving mass of carriage and loaded tray $\\Mass$ = <code>[PackagingCell.mass_str]</code> and nominal speed $\\SpeedAtDetection$ = <code>[PackagingCell.v_nom_str]</code> (Illustrative)</li>\n<li>Clearance <code>[PackagingCell.clearance_str]</code>, less <code>[PackagingCell.reserve_str]</code> reserved for uncertainty and residual clearance, leaves a stopping budget $\\StoppingDistanceBudget$ = <code>[PackagingCell.stop_budget_str]</code> (Illustrative)</li>\n<li>Braking lower bound $\\CredibleBrakingDeceleration$ = <code>[PackagingCell.deceleration_str]</code> after the delay, for whichever brake the stop command engages (Illustrative; a bound to qualify under load, pad wear, and rail conditions, not a motor's peak rating)</li>\n<li>Delay from closure to full braking $\\PrebrakeDelay$: <code>[PackagingCell.exposure_str]</code> to a usable image, including frame phase and exposure, <code>[PackagingCell.processing_str]</code> for transfer, inference, scheduling, and admission, <code>[PackagingCell.bus_str]</code> for command delivery, and <code>[PackagingCell.onset_str]</code> for brake buildup, in all <code>[PackagingCell.delay_str]</code> (Illustrative upper bounds)</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Math</strong>:</p>\n<ol class=\"cs-list\">\n<li><strong>Nominal stop</strong> (@eq-body-stopping-distance, margins held in the reserve): $\\StoppingDistance = \\SpeedAtDetection\\PrebrakeDelay + \\SpeedAtDetection^2/2\\CredibleBrakingDeceleration$ = <code>[PackagingCell.v_nom_str]</code> $\\times$ <code>[PackagingCell.delay_str]</code> $+$ (<code>[PackagingCell.v_nom_str]</code>)$^2/(2 \\times$ <code>[PackagingCell.deceleration_str]</code>) = <code>[PackagingCell.blind_nom_str]</code> $+$ <code>[PackagingCell.brake_nom_str]</code> = <code>[PackagingCell.stop_nom_str]</code>, a deficit of <code>[PackagingCell.deficit_str]</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Speed ceiling</strong> (@eq-body-velocity-ceiling with $\\StoppingDistanceBudget$ as the available distance): $\\CredibleBrakingDeceleration\\PrebrakeDelay$ = <code>[PackagingCell.a_tau_str]</code> and $2\\CredibleBrakingDeceleration{}\\StoppingDistanceBudget$ = <code>[PackagingCell.two_a_d_str]</code>, and the square root of (<code>[PackagingCell.a_tau_str]</code>)$^2$ $+$ <code>[PackagingCell.two_a_d_str]</code> is <code>[PackagingCell.root_term_str]</code>. The positive root is $\\SpeedBound$ = <code>[PackagingCell.root_term_str]</code> $-$ <code>[PackagingCell.a_tau_str]</code> = <code>[PackagingCell.v_ceiling_str]</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Candidate mode</strong> at <code>[PackagingCell.v_clamp_str]</code>, below the ceiling: <code>[PackagingCell.blind_clamp_str]</code> of delay travel $+$ <code>[PackagingCell.brake_clamp_str]</code> of braking = <code>[PackagingCell.stop_clamp_str]</code>, leaving <code>[PackagingCell.remaining_str]</code> before the stop line. Translational energy $\\KineticEnergy = \\Mass \\SpeedAtDetection^2/2$ falls from <code>[PackagingCell.energy_nom_str]</code> to <code>[PackagingCell.energy_clamp_str]</code>, a reduction of <code>[PackagingCell.energy_reduction_str]</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Result</strong>: At nominal speed the carriage reaches the gate before braking even begins. The budget admits at most <code>[PackagingCell.v_ceiling_str]</code>, and the <code>[PackagingCell.v_clamp_str]</code> candidate keeps more than the allocated reserve.</p>\n<p><strong>Systems insight</strong>: At these speeds delay travel, not braking, exhausts the clearance. The <code>[PackagingCell.delay_str]</code> chain alone carries the nominal carriage <code>[PackagingCell.blind_nom_str]</code>, farther than the stop line, so the condition $\\StoppingDistance \\le \\ClearDistance$ of principle \\ref{pri-vol4-irreversibility} is met first by lowering speed or shortening the chain from closure to braking. The carriage's speed limit belongs to that whole chain, not to its brake alone.</p>\n<p>The slower mode is therefore worth qualifying. Cutting speed to a third shrinks the two terms of the stop unequally (@fig-18-packaging-cell). Delay travel is linear in speed and falls threefold, from <code>[PackagingCell.blind_nom_str]</code> to <code>[PackagingCell.blind_clamp_str]</code>; braking distance is quadratic and falls ninefold, to <code>[PackagingCell.brake_clamp_str]</code>. At the restricted speed the delay chain, not the brake, therefore sets most of the stop, which is why the next question is which premise moves the ceiling most.</p>\n<pre class=\"cs-code\"><code>#| echo: false\n# ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n# │ PACKAGING CELL FIGURE LABELS\n# ├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n# │ Context: @fig-18-packaging-cell (SVG labels are hand-typed).\n# │ Goal:    Keep every delay and distance drawn in the figure equal to the\n# │          PackagingCell values the prose prints.\n# │ Show:    Nothing; the cell only guards the drawn labels.\n# │ How:     Compare each drawn label (ms, mm, m/s) with its computed value.\n# └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nfrom mlsysim.core.units import meter, mm, ms, second\n\nclass PackagingCellFigure:\n    &quot;&quot;&quot;Values drawn in fig18_packaging_cell.svg.&quot;&quot;&quot;\n\n    _P = PackagingCell\n    _ms = lambda q: q.to(ms).magnitude\n    _mm = lambda q: q.to(mm).magnitude\n    _mps = lambda q: q.to(meter / second).magnitude\n    drawn = [\n        (&quot;image&quot;, 16.0, _ms(_P.exposure), 1e-9),\n        (&quot;transfer, inference, admission&quot;, 36.0, _ms(_P.processing), 1e-9),\n        (&quot;bus&quot;, 2.0, _ms(_P.bus), 1e-9),\n        (&quot;onset&quot;, 10.0, _ms(_P.onset), 1e-9),\n        (&quot;total delay&quot;, 64.0, _ms(_P.delay), 1e-9),\n        (&quot;clearance&quot;, 60.0, _mm(_P.clearance), 1e-9),\n        (&quot;reserve start&quot;, 45.0, _mm(_P.stop_budget), 1e-9),\n        (&quot;nominal speed&quot;, 1.5, _mps(_P.v_nom), 1e-9),\n        (&quot;restricted speed&quot;, 0.5, _mps(_P.v_clamp), 1e-9),\n        (&quot;nominal delay travel&quot;, 96.0, _mm(_P.blind_nom), 1e-9),\n        (&quot;nominal braking&quot;, 75.0, _mm(_P.brake_nom), 1e-9),\n        (&quot;nominal stop&quot;, 171.0, _mm(_P.stop_nom), 1e-9),\n        (&quot;restricted delay travel&quot;, 32.0, _mm(_P.blind_clamp), 1e-9),\n        (&quot;restricted stop&quot;, 40.3, _mm(_P.stop_clamp), 0.05),\n        (&quot;restricted spare&quot;, 19.7, _mm(_P.remaining), 0.05),\n    ]\n    for _name, _label, _value, _tol in drawn:\n        check(abs(_label - _value) &lt; _tol, f&quot;packaging-cell figure label for {_name} is stale ({_label} vs {_value})&quot;)\n]```\n\n::: {#fig-18-packaging-cell fig-env=&quot;figure&quot; fig-pos=&quot;t&quot; fig-cap=&quot;**Three machine classes in one packaging cell**: (a) The carriage is the moving mass whose impact deforms carton and frame, the insertion head applies the contact force that can buckle a carton wall, and the drive dissipates heat that ages winding insulation. (b) The assumed delay chain from gate closure to full braking totals `[PackagingCell.delay_str]`. (c) At `[PackagingCell.v_nom_str]` the carriage travels `[PackagingCell.blind_nom_str]` before braking begins and stops after `[PackagingCell.stop_nom_str]`, far beyond the `[PackagingCell.clearance_str]` stop line; at the restricted `[PackagingCell.v_clamp_str]` it stops in `[PackagingCell.stop_clamp_str]`, clear of the `[PackagingCell.reserve_str]` reserve. All inputs are hypothetical design allocations, not measurements.&quot; fig-alt=&quot;Three panels. Panel a is a side-view schematic of a linear track with a drive box at the left end, a carriage carrying a carton, an insertion head pressing down on the carton, and a gate with a red dashed stop line at the right; labels name the drive as thermal dissipation with insulation aging, the carriage as moving mass whose impact deforms carton and frame, and the insertion head as contact force that buckles the carton wall. Panel b is a horizontal delay bar of four segments, image 16 ms, transfer inference and admission 36 ms, bus 2 ms, and brake onset 10 ms, totaling 64 ms from closure. Panel c shows two stopping-distance bars on a millimeter axis from 0 to 180 with a red dashed stop line at 60 mm and a hatched reserve band just before it. At 1.5 m/s the bar has 96 mm of delay travel and 75 mm of braking, reaching 171 mm, with the part beyond the stop line shaded red. At 0.5 m/s the bar has 32 mm of delay travel and a short braking segment, stopping at 40.3 mm with 19.7 mm to spare.&quot;}\n\n:::\n\nThe positive root also shows how the ceiling responds to each premise. Halving the processing allocation to `[PackagingCell.processing_half_str]` raises the ceiling to `[PackagingCell.v_ceiling_fast_proc_str]`; because delay travel dominates this stop, reducing that allocation recovers substantial speed margin. Halving the qualified braking bound to `[PackagingCell.deceleration_half_str]` lowers the ceiling to `[PackagingCell.v_ceiling_half_decel_str]`, below the candidate `[PackagingCell.v_clamp_str]` mode, which then no longer fits. The two premises play different roles. Shorter latency is an opportunity for throughput, whereas a braking shortfall invalidates the mode outright, which is why the loaded deceleration must be qualified under worst-case conditions before the cell is commissioned. The stopping equation fixes the relationship; only measurements on the assembled cell can establish that the latency budget, the deceleration bound, and the reserve hold.\n\n::: {.column-margin .margin-pointer}\n↩ This speed ceiling, the positive root of the stopping equation, is established for the mobile base in @sec-body-dynamic-velocity-ceilings.\n:::\n\nThe second law evaluates the competence of the cell&#x27;s learned proposers in the closed loop. This packaging cell runs three distinct learned proposers. A vision model infers container contents to propose carriage transit speeds (fragile or liquid contents tolerate gentler accelerations and stops than solid goods). A multimodal network estimates cardboard thickness to propose insertion forces and dwell durations. An electro-thermal estimator proposes acceleration profiles to manage motor winding temperatures. Each proposer&#x27;s actions alter what it observes next. Throttling carriage speed changes camera frame timing and motion blur, extending tool dwell changes the material deformation that later thickness estimates read, and reshaping acceleration profiles changes the temperature trajectory the thermal estimator measures. Evidence of proposer competence therefore holds only for the state distribution sampled during closed-loop evaluation, and the question is whether that distribution matches the conditions admitted at runtime. A safety restriction imposed after training shifts that distribution. If closed-loop evaluation sampled only the nominal transit speed of `[PackagingCell.v_nom_str]`, clamping speed to `[PackagingCell.v_clamp_str]` pushes the vision model into visual dynamics it never encountered during evaluation, so the restriction that secures the stopping distance also opens a regime without evidence. The engineering team must either evaluate the policy under derated conditions before release or record the unverified regime as an open premise in the release sign-off.\n\n::: {.column-margin .margin-pointer}\n↩ A policy whose actions choose its next observations drives the endogenous covariate shift established in @sec-data-covariate-shift.\n:::\n\nClosing this gap requires matching the data-collection and evaluation campaign to the restricted operating envelope. Expert demonstrations must span the admitted transport velocities, insertion forces, and thermal regimes, preserving complete site metadata in the provenance record. Once trained, the candidate proposer must undergo closed-loop trials on the physical cell to confirm that it completes the packaging task under those conditions, including during interventions by the permission path. If the cell cannot maintain acceptable task performance within its physical bounds, engineers must revise the task specification, the proposer architecture, or the mechanical plant before re-evaluating the modified system. Calculating a speed ceiling tests the transport stopping budget under its stated premises; qualifying those premises and demonstrating closed-loop task performance are both required for a viable packaging cell.\n\n::: {.column-margin .margin-pointer}\n↩ Why proposer evidence must come from the closed loop the machine will actually run is established in @sec-evaluation-closed-loop-dynamics.\n:::\n\nThe third law places these proposed actions beneath an architectural authority split that the proposers cannot bypass, delay, or corrupt. The permission path prevents unverified neural proposals from reaching physical actuators directly. In this packaging cell, an independent kinematic monitor runs at `[PackagingCell.check_rate_str]`, reading the carriage&#x27;s optical encoders and a rangefinder aimed at the gate, and admits a carriage motion command only when the measured clearance supports the qualified stopping distance. This monitor is the admission stage of the camera path in notebook \\ref{nbk-frontier-packaging-delay-wall}: the camera reports that a closure has begun, and the monitor admits the stop. Its work is therefore part of the `[PackagingCell.processing_str]` processing allocation, and a `[PackagingCell.check_rate_str]` tick bounds only how long a command waits for the next check, not the transfer, inference, and scheduling that come before it.\n\nWhile the software kinematic check confines normal operation to the derated velocity mode, it does not carry the cell&#x27;s formal protection claims. In an industrial deployment, harm-prevention claims rest on hardware-certified drive safety functions codified under IEC 61800-5-2 [@iec61800part52] and mapped for warehouse automation in @tbl-12-certified-functions. The restricted transport mode maps to safely limited speed (SLS) on the carriage drive, an independent hardware function that continuously monitors motor velocity and triggers an emergency stop if the limit is exceeded. The emergency response executes as a ramp-monitored safe stop 1 (SS1) terminating in safe torque off (STO), with safe brake control (SBC) engaging mechanical holding brakes at rest. Each safety function requires a validated timing budget and an integrity metric. Under ISO 13849-1 [@iso13849part1], safety functions are classified by performance level (PL), which bounds their average probability of dangerous failure per hour according to the cell&#x27;s risk assessment. The camera-triggered stop budgeted in notebook \\ref{nbk-frontier-packaging-delay-wall} is the application-level path announced before it, not a protective safety function. A safety-rated optical gate sensor feeding the drive&#x27;s SS1 input directly replaces the frame-exposure and neural-inference allocations with its certified hardware response time, while command delivery and brake onset still contribute to $\\PrebrakeDelay$ in the physical stopping model.\n\nPhysical safety limits must govern the insertion tool and actuator drives just as strictly as carriage motion. Setting a mechanical force limiter to `[PackagingCell.force_cap_str]` is a nominal design choice rather than proof that every parcel resists crushing. Physical tests must verify that peak transmitted forces, including dynamic overshoot during contact impact, stay below the yield strength of the weakest admitted carton and tab geometry. Similarly, setting a winding thermistor trip threshold at `[PackagingCell.temperature_trip_str]` and a stall detection timeout of `[PackagingCell.stall_timeout_str]` provides basic supervisory cutoffs rather than a certified thermal envelope. Safe continuous operation depends on drive current profiles, winding resistance, heat capacity, cooling airflow, and sensor thermal lag. Because internal winding temperatures can surge before external housing sensors register the heat, as modeled in @sec-body-thermal-capacity, engineers cannot validate a sustained duty cycle from trip thresholds alone without measuring the thermal response under load.\n\nHigh-rate deceleration also transfers mechanical kinetic energy back into the electrical drive system. During regenerative braking, the decelerating motor returns power to the inverter DC bus. A dynamic brake chopper and power resistor must absorb this energy before bus voltage exceeds the inverter trip threshold of `[PackagingCell.bus_trip_str]`, following the bus energy balance in @sec-body-regenerative-surges. The energy sink must handle repeated stops across a full shift rather than an isolated emergency event. In addition, regenerative braking requires an energized inverter stage throughout deceleration, whereas the carriage&#x27;s electromechanical track brake relies on its own spring-actuated mechanism. Logic power hold-up alone supplies neither regenerative braking torque nor friction-brake clamping force. A thermal fault or electrical shutdown that cuts drive power disables regenerative braking, leaving deceleration entirely to the mechanical brake. The notebook&#x27;s `[PackagingCell.deceleration_str]` bound therefore holds after a power cut only if the spring-applied track brake is itself qualified to it. Otherwise the power-cut case has a lower deceleration, a longer stop, and a lower speed ceiling of its own. System designers must ensure that safety-directed power cuts preserve a qualified deceleration and holding response across the declared operating states and fault conditions.\n\nThe fourth law synthesizes these engineering decisions into a release argument. The cell&#x27;s release manifest must validate rangefinder coverage, gate clearance margins, braking deceleration, and sustained task throughput as one connected system. Equally important, the manifest explicitly records any claims that physical evidence cannot support. To check whether the tabs have latched, the engineering team evaluates a vision model that inspects surface reflections for container tab engagement. Under industrial overhead lighting, glare from tape and crinkled plastic packaging produces bright reflections that mimic latch defects while hiding genuine tab misalignments. Because the cell lacks an independent physical measurement, such as a tactile switch or a laser profile scanner, that separates engaged tabs from misaligned flaps under glare, the sign-off claim lacks a qualified detector. Recording this limitation as detectorless and unsupported in the residual-claims register (@sec-frontier-residual-claims-register) is not a documentation failure but an accurate statement of what the physical evidence justifies.\n\nThe stopping analysis therefore supports a *candidate restricted mode* for carriage transit at `[PackagingCell.v_clamp_str]`, provided that the `[PackagingCell.delay_str]` end-to-end latency, `[PackagingCell.deceleration_str]` braking deceleration, `[PackagingCell.clearance_str]` physical clearance, and uncertainty reserve are validated together on the physical cell. Insertion forces and thermal duty cycles remain restricted until physical testing confirms material crush limits, sensor thermal lag, and independent fault paths. Autonomous package sign-off is refused because glare hides tab engagement from the vision system and the cell lacks an independent verification sensor. A qualified tactile or laser engagement sensor could support autonomous sign-off; mandatory downstream manual inspection instead supports a commercial deployment that makes no autonomous sign-off claim. An analytical throughput model cannot substitute for that physical evidence. Each law&#x27;s answer constrains the next. The stopping mechanics fix a transport envelope, the envelope fixes the regime that data collection and evaluation must cover, the runtime monitors enforce that envelope, and the release argument restricts the cell wherever a claim lacks support.\n\nThe same audit extends beyond the three machine classes of the packaging cell into fluid and chemical process systems, where the irreversible consequence may be reagent already delivered or heat already transferred rather than damage from a moving mass. Consider a learned proposer that sets the opening of a dosing valve feeding a reaction tank, with a high-level trip that commands the valve shut before the tank overfills. The first law asks what reagent has already been committed. If the shutoff command arrives after a delay $\\PrebrakeDelay$ at a volumetric flow rate $\\VolumetricFlowRate$, fluid continues to discharge. The discharge adds roughly $\\VolumetricFlowRate\\PrebrakeDelay$ to the volume delivered before the valve even begins to move, the fluidic analog of the delay travel $\\SpeedAtDetection\\PrebrakeDelay$ in the stopping distance. The valve&#x27;s closing stroke then adds the fluidic analog of braking distance, and both must fit within the headroom above the trip level, as the clearance did for the carriage. Stored thermodynamic and hydraulic energy also complicates the fallback. A pressurized accumulator, a cryogenic transfer line, or a heated pressure vessel retains stored energy after electronic control power is cut. As with a balancing biped, whose fall begins the moment its torque is removed, de-energizing the controller does not by itself produce a safe state. The design must therefore name a qualified physical sink, such as a spring-loaded pressure relief bypass, to dissipate that energy. The remaining laws apply in the same way. Policy evaluation must sample the throttled flow regimes that the safety limits enforce, the permission path must guarantee valve closure within the allowable volume tolerance, and sign-off must rest on measured valve slew rates and sensor response times. In every case the machine&#x27;s operating claims hold only as far as its physical evidence reaches, and a physical state that stays unobserved until the execution deadline marks where that evidence ends.\n\n::: {#chk-frontier-packaging-transfer .callout-checkpoint title=&quot;Transferring the four-law audit&quot;}\n\nThe packaging cell demonstrates how the four-law audit organizes system design for an entirely novel machine, anchoring every operational claim to physical bounds and empirical measurements.\n\n- [ ] **Speed-ceiling estimate**: Suppose physical load testing qualifies the carriage&#x27;s braking bound at only `[PackagingCell.deceleration_check_str]`, assuming the same `[PackagingCell.delay_str]` total delay and `[PackagingCell.stop_budget_str]` stopping budget. Estimate the updated velocity ceiling from the positive root of the stopping equation, and determine whether the candidate `[PackagingCell.v_clamp_str]` mode still fits within the updated stopping budget under the remaining assumed inputs.\n- [ ] **Machine classes**: For the carriage, the insertion head, and the linear drive motors, identify each component&#x27;s physical machine class, the irreversible physical state transition it can produce, and the governing physical boundary.\n- [ ] **Derated evaluation**: Explain why imposing a runtime velocity restriction after training induces an endogenous covariate shift for the vision proposer. What two engineering options can close this evaluation gap before operational sign-off?\n- [ ] **Sign-off verdict**: What specific physical sensing evidence would be required to transition autonomous package sign-off from refused to supported, and why does expanding the training dataset with more glare-corrupted images fail to resolve the underlying detector deficiency?\n\n:::\n\n## The Epistemic Frontier {#sec-frontier-epistemic-limits}\n\nA packaging cell&#x27;s camera can confirm an arm&#x27;s position and approve a transport motion without verifying that a hidden mechanical tab has engaged. Similarly, a warehouse robot&#x27;s lidar and cameras can clear a navigation path across an aisle without determining whether the concrete floor provides enough traction to brake. These gaps are epistemic limits: boundaries on what the available sensors and analytical arguments can justify. Additional training can improve a policy&#x27;s action proposals, but machine learning cannot manufacture an unmeasured physical state. Systems engineers must therefore determine whether justifying the next operating claim requires broader training experience or a physical measurement absent from the existing sensor suite.\n\nConsider an aisle where a pallet jack with a leaking hydraulic line has just crossed, leaving a thin, clear film of fluid on polished concrete. The mobile manipulator&#x27;s stopping budget assumes the floor supplies enough surface friction for credible deceleration, which dry industrial concrete reliably does. Under an oil film, however, available traction collapses, yet neither standard navigation cameras nor time-of-flight lidars can tell the clear fluid apart from dry concrete. In this failure mode the safety architecture runs exactly as specified. The software permission path meets every deadline, chunk leases expire on schedule, and the drive controller applies braking torque right on time. Even so, the robot slides past its stop line and collides at the rack end. The failure comes not from software latency or actuator lag, but from an unmonitored premise, the local wheel-floor friction coefficient before braking, and the stopping budget prices that premise.\n</code></pre>\n<p>#| echo: false #| warning: false #| message: false</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ THE OIL FILM ON THE AISLE</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h1\">├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Context: @sec-frontier-epistemic-limits (the premise behind the aisle</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          speed), @sec-frontier-residual-claims-register (the register</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          entries and @tbl-18-claims-register),</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          @sec-frontier-failures-without-detectors (detection latency against the spare margin),</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          @sec-frontier-fallacies-pitfalls (the vision-only pitfall).</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h1\">│</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Goal:    Show that the 1.3 m/s normal mode always rested on a friction</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          floor that no sensor on the machine reports.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Show:    the friction the credible deceleration needs; the stop on a clear</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          film against the clear distance; the inspected-floor ceiling and</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          restricted mode; the zero-failure closure test; the time the spare</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          margin buys; the film-detector range at a chosen latency; how much</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          a time-form action deadline over-reserves against the distance form. The walking-person ceiling is Ch12's</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          (@sec-enforcement-composite-budget) and is cited, not recomputed.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ How:     credible deceleration a = min(a_brake, mu g); the finished budget</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          (jerk-limited S-curve stop, all three overheads) at the aisle speed</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          from Scenarios.WarehouseFinishedStop* (dry, inspected floor, oil</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          film), jerk bound = the reference C2 polynomial's peak jerk at the</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          drive limit (@sec-planning-base-stopping-budget); the film-detector</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          range and closure test from Scenarios.WarehouseFilmDetector*.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h1\">└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>from mlsysim import * from mlsysim.physics.drivetrain import calc_required_friction from mlsysim.core.units import dimensionless, meter, millimeter, second, standard_gravity</p>\n<p>RM = Embodied.MobileManipulator.WarehouseMobileManipulator AISLE = Embodied.Scenario.WarehouseAisle</p>\n<p>class OilFilm:</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 1. LOAD (Constants &amp; Parameters) ─────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>g = (1 <em> standard_gravity).to(meter / second</em>*2)  # category: measured (physical constant, standard gravity) a_brake = AISLE.a_brake  # category: illustrative (credible deceleration, dry floor) mu_dry = AISLE.mu_dry  # category: illustrative (dry concrete) mu_insp = AISLE.mu_inspected_floor  # category: illustrative (inspected floor) mu_oil = AISLE.mu_oil_film  # category: illustrative (clear hydraulic film) v_aisle = AISLE.v_aisle  # category: chosen (normal-mode aisle speed) d_clear = AISLE.d_clear  # category: illustrative (rack-end clear distance) tau_delay = AISLE.tau_delay  # category: derived (pre-brake delay, excluding Delta_da) delta_da = AISLE.t_dispatch_to_admission  # category: illustrative (allocation of @sec-placement-dispatch-admission, not yet measured) overhead = AISLE.fixed_overhead  # category: derived (delta_loc + delta_margin + eps_track) v_h = AISLE.v_human_approach  # category: datasheet (ISO 13855 walking speed) _STOP = Scenarios.WarehouseFinishedStopAtAisleSpeed  # finished budget, dry floor _INSP = Scenarios.WarehouseFinishedStopInspectedFloor  # finished budget, inspected floor _FILM = Scenarios.WarehouseFinishedStopOilFilm  # finished budget, clear oil film _DETECT = Scenarios.WarehouseFilmDetectorRange  # dry budget plus the detector's latency _CLOSURE = Scenarios.WarehouseFilmDetectorClosure  # zero-miss closure campaign v_restricted = Scenarios.WarehouseFinishedStopRestricted.speed  # category: chosen (restricted mode on inspected floors) p_miss_target = _CLOSURE.target_rate  # category: chosen (detector miss-rate bound for closure) n_contrast = _CLOSURE.trials  # category: illustrative (a smaller zero-miss campaign, for contrast) confidence = _CLOSURE.confidence  # category: chosen (one-sided confidence level) t_detect = _DETECT.added_delay  # category: chosen (film-detector sensing and inference latency, for the range condition) mu_check = 0.15  # category: illustrative (a measured floor, for the checkpoint estimate)</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 2. EXECUTE (Compute) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>mu_min = calc_required_friction(a_brake) a_dry = min(a_brake, mu_dry <em> g) a_insp = min(a_brake, mu_insp </em> g) a_oil = min(a_brake, mu_oil * g)</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h1\">Jerk bound: the reference C2 polynomial's peak jerk at the drive limit; the</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h1\">floor caps the deceleration, while the jerk bound belongs to the load.</h1>\n<p>dry, insp, film = _STOP.result, _INSP.result, _FILM.result j_brake = dry.jerk.to(meter / second**3)</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h1\">finished budget at the aisle speed: v tau + S-curve stop + overhead</h1>\n<p>budget_aisle = dry.required_distance.to(meter) spare_aisle = dry.spare.to(millimeter) t_latest = dry.delay_spare.to(ms) reserve_da = (t_latest - delta_da).to(ms)  # timing allowance left after the Delta_da allocation stop_oil = film.required_distance.to(meter)</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h1\">Visible terms of the finished budget (trapezoidal regime: v^2/2a + v a/2j, @sec-enforcement-composite-budget)</h1>\n<p>v_tau = dry.delay_travel.to(millimeter) brake_dry = dry.braking_travel.to(millimeter) ramp_dry = dry.jerk_ramp_travel.to(millimeter) brake_oil = film.braking_travel.to(millimeter) ramp_oil = film.jerk_ramp_travel.to(millimeter)</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h1\">ceilings: the largest speed whose finished budget fits the clear distance</h1>\n<p>ceiling_dry = dry.speed_ceiling ceiling_insp = insp.speed_ceiling</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h1\">film detector: range needed so the dry-floor stop ends before the film's leading edge</h1>\n<p>d_detect_min = (_DETECT.result.required_distance + _DETECT.result.added_delay_travel).to(meter)</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h1\">action deadline, time form vs distance form, for the S-curve stop at the aisle speed</h1>\n<p>t_brake_aisle = dry.stop_time.to(ms) d_brake_aisle = dry.scurve_travel.to(millimeter) t_brake_equiv = (d_brake_aisle / v_aisle).to(ms)  # time at the unchanged speed that the stop's distance represents t_overreserve = (t_brake_aisle - t_brake_equiv).to(ms) d_overreserve = (v_aisle * t_overreserve).to(millimeter)</p>\n<p>n_closure = _CLOSURE.result.trials_for_target p_miss_contrast = _CLOSURE.result.rate_upper_exact</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 3. GUARD (Invariants) ────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>check(abs(mu_min - 0.204) &lt; 5e-4, f\"Friction floor should be 0.204, got {mu_min}.\") check(a_dry == a_brake and mu_dry &gt; mu_min, \"On dry concrete the brakes, not friction, must bind.\") check(mu_oil &lt; mu_min and mu_insp &lt; mu_min, \"On the film and on an inspected floor friction must bind.\") check(abs(a_insp.to(meter / second<strong>2).magnitude - 1.1768) &lt; 5e-4, f\"Inspected-floor deceleration should be 1.18 m/s², got {a_insp}.\") check(abs(j_brake.magnitude - 64.0 / 9.0) &lt; 1e-9, f\"Jerk bound should be the reference polynomial's 7.11 m/s^3 peak, got {j_brake}.\") check(abs(budget_aisle.to(millimeter).magnitude - 968.9925) &lt; 5e-3, f\"Budget at 1.3 m/s should be 968.99 mm, got {budget_aisle}.\") check(abs(spare_aisle.to(millimeter).magnitude - 131.0075) &lt; 5e-3, f\"Spare at 1.3 m/s should be 131.01 mm, got {spare_aisle}.\") check(abs(t_latest.to(ms).magnitude - 100.775) &lt; 5e-3, f\"T_latest should be 100.8 ms, got {t_latest}.\") check(reserve_da.magnitude &gt; 0, \"The Delta_da allocation must leave positive reserve at the aisle speed.\") check(t_detect + delta_da &gt; t_latest, \"The chosen detector latency and the Delta_da allocation cannot both fit the allowance.\") check(abs(ceiling_dry.to(meter / second).magnitude - 1.4367) &lt; 5e-5, f\"Dry-floor ceiling should be 1.4367 m/s, got {ceiling_dry}.\") check(abs(ceiling_insp.to(meter / second).magnitude - 1.2309) &lt; 5e-5, f\"Inspected-floor ceiling should be 1.2309 m/s, got {ceiling_insp}.\") check( v_restricted &lt; ceiling_insp &lt; v_aisle &lt; ceiling_dry, \"Restricted mode must sit under the inspected ceiling, and the aisle speed under the dry ceiling.\", ) check(abs(stop_oil.to(meter).magnitude - 2.13) &lt; 5e-3, f\"Finished budget on the film should be 2.13 m, got {stop_oil}.\") check(film.deceleration == a_oil and insp.deceleration == a_insp, \"The scenarios' floor-limited decelerations must match equation 1.\") check(abs(a_oil.to(meter / second</strong>2).magnitude - 0.49) &lt; 5e-3, f\"Film deceleration should be 0.49 m/s², got {a_oil}.\") check(stop_oil &gt; d_clear, f\"On the film the incomplete budget from the aisle speed must already exceed the clear distance, got {stop_oil}.\") check(n_closure == 2995, f\"Zero-failure closure test should need 2,995 trials, got {n_closure}.\") check(abs(d_detect_min.to(millimeter).magnitude - 1098.9925) &lt; 5e-3, f\"Film-detector range should be 1.10 m, got {d_detect_min}.\") check(abs(t_brake_aisle.magnitude - 931.25) &lt; 1e-6, f\"S-curve stop from 1.3 m/s should take 931.25 ms, got {t_brake_aisle}.\") check(abs(d_brake_aisle.magnitude - 605.3125) &lt; 1e-6, f\"S-curve stop from 1.3 m/s should cover 605.31 mm, got {d_brake_aisle}.\") check(abs(t_overreserve.magnitude - 465.625) &lt; 1e-6, f\"Time form over-reserves 465.6 ms, got {t_overreserve}.\") check(d_overreserve &gt; spare_aisle, \"The time form's over-reservation exceeds the whole spare margin.\") check(v_aisle &gt; a_dry<strong>2 / j_brake and v_aisle &gt; a_oil</strong>2 / j_brake, \"Both stops must be in the trapezoidal regime for the shown terms.\") check(abs((v_tau + brake_dry + ramp_dry + overhead - budget_aisle).to(millimeter).magnitude) &lt; 1e-6, \"Shown dry terms must sum to the budget.\") check( abs((v_tau + brake_oil + ramp_oil + overhead - stop_oil).to(millimeter).magnitude) &lt; 1e-6, \"Shown film terms must sum to the film budget.\", ) check(mu_check * g &lt; a_brake and mu_check &gt; mu_insp, \"The checkpoint floor must bind and lie above the inspected floor.\") check(abs(p_miss_contrast - 0.002991) &lt; 5e-7, f\"1,000 zero-miss trials should bound the miss rate at 0.002991, got {p_miss_contrast}.\")</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h1\">margin_friction_floor_deceleration_knee.svg fig-alt hand-types 2 m/s², 0.204, 0.60, 0.12, 1.18, 0.05 and 0.49</h1>\n<p>check(abs(a_brake.to(meter / second**2).magnitude - 2.0) &lt; 1e-9, f\"Credible deceleration should be 2 m/s², got {a_brake}.\") check(abs(mu_dry - 0.60) &lt; 1e-9 and abs(mu_insp - 0.12) &lt; 1e-9 and abs(mu_oil - 0.05) &lt; 1e-9, \"Floor coefficients should be 0.60, 0.12 and 0.05.\")</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h1\">margin_stop_time_overreserve_envelope.svg fig-alt hand-types 931.2, 465.6, 465.6 and 100.8 ms (guards above)</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 4. OUTPUT (Formatting) ───────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>a_brake_str = fmt_acceleration(a_brake, precision=0) mu_min_str = fmt(mu_min, precision=3) mu_dry_str = fmt(mu_dry, precision=2) mu_insp_str = fmt(mu_insp, precision=2) mu_oil_str = fmt(mu_oil, precision=2) a_insp_str = fmt_acceleration(a_insp, precision=2) a_oil_str = fmt_acceleration(a_oil, precision=2) v_aisle_str = fmt_velocity(v_aisle, precision=1) d_clear_str = fmt_length(d_clear, unit=meter, precision=2) budget_aisle_str = fmt_length(budget_aisle, unit=millimeter, precision=1) spare_aisle_str = fmt_length(spare_aisle, unit=millimeter, precision=1) t_latest_str = fmt_latency(t_latest, unit=ms, precision=1) delta_da_str = fmt_latency(delta_da, unit=ms, precision=1) reserve_da_str = fmt_latency(reserve_da, unit=ms, precision=1) stop_oil_str = fmt_length(stop_oil, unit=meter, precision=2) ceiling_insp_str = fmt_velocity(ceiling_insp, precision=2) v_restricted_str = fmt_velocity(v_restricted, precision=0) n_closure_str = fmt_int(n_closure, commas=True) n_contrast_str = fmt_int(n_contrast, commas=True) p_miss_contrast_str = fmt(p_miss_contrast, precision=6) p_miss_target_pct_str = fmt_percent(p_miss_target, precision=1, style=\"prose\") p_miss_target_sym_str = fmt_percent(p_miss_target, precision=1, style=\"symbol\") confidence_pct_str = fmt_percent(confidence, precision=0, style=\"prose\") confidence_sym_str = fmt_percent(confidence, precision=0, style=\"symbol\") v_h_str = fmt_velocity(v_h, precision=1) t_detect_str = fmt_latency(t_detect, unit=ms, precision=0) d_detect_min_str = fmt_length(d_detect_min, unit=meter, precision=2) t_brake_aisle_str = fmt_latency(t_brake_aisle, unit=ms, precision=1) t_brake_equiv_str = fmt_latency(t_brake_equiv, unit=ms, precision=1) t_overreserve_str = fmt_latency(t_overreserve, unit=ms, precision=1) d_overreserve_str = fmt_length(d_overreserve, unit=millimeter, precision=1) g_str = fmt_acceleration(g, precision=2) tau_delay_str = fmt_latency(tau_delay, unit=ms, precision=1) j_brake_str = fmt_qty(j_brake, unit=meter / second**3, precision=2) overhead_str = fmt_length(overhead, unit=millimeter, precision=0) v_tau_str = fmt_length(v_tau, unit=millimeter, precision=1) brake_dry_str = fmt_length(brake_dry, unit=millimeter, precision=1) ramp_dry_str = fmt_length(ramp_dry, unit=millimeter, precision=1) brake_oil_str = fmt_length(brake_oil, unit=millimeter, precision=1) ramp_oil_str = fmt_length(ramp_oil, unit=millimeter, precision=1) mu_check_str = fmt(mu_check, precision=2) ]```</p>\n<p>The claim adjudication in @sec-release-argument-plant-alignment already encountered this premise: claim C3 (braking traction) was refused on this book's own evidence and deferred to this chapter as an unresolved premise. The credible deceleration $\\CredibleBrakingDeceleration$ that @sec-body credited to the brakes was always conditional on the floor. Made explicit, that condition says the deceleration a machine actually has available is the smaller of what its brakes can deliver and what tire-floor traction can transmit: $$\\AvailableDeceleration = \\min(\\CredibleBrakingDeceleration, \\CoulombFrictionCoefficient \\Gravity).$$ {#eq-frontier-credible-deceleration} The friction term is the traction ceiling of @sec-body (@fig-02-braking-fbd and @sec-body-electrical-time-constants), $\\CoulombFrictionCoefficient \\DrivenWheelLoadFraction \\Gravity$, taken with all of the weight on the driven wheels ($\\DrivenWheelLoadFraction = 1$). That is the case that sets the friction floor, the same optimistic all-weight screen that claim C3 was adjudicated against. At the drive wheels' rated load share the floor is higher, as that notebook computes, so every friction margin that follows is the optimistic one. On an oily surface, interface friction rather than motor torque becomes the binding constraint.</p>\n<p><strong>Problem</strong>: A clear hydraulic film lies across the aisle. Does the known-component stop from the candidate <code>[OilFilm.v_aisle_str]</code> aisle speed still fit the rack end's <code>[OilFilm.d_clear_str]</code> clear distance?</p>\n<p><strong>Variables</strong>:</p>\n<ul class=\"cs-list\">\n<li>Credible deceleration $\\CredibleBrakingDeceleration$ = <code>[OilFilm.a_brake_str]</code> (Illustrative, @sec-body-kinetic-momentum) and $\\Gravity$ = <code>[OilFilm.g_str]</code> (Defined standard-gravity constant)</li>\n<li>Floor friction $\\CoulombFrictionCoefficient$ = <code>[OilFilm.mu_dry_str]</code> on dry industrial concrete and <code>[OilFilm.mu_oil_str]</code> under the film (both Illustrative; see the Reader Guide)</li>\n<li>Aisle speed $\\SpeedAtDetection$ = <code>[OilFilm.v_aisle_str]</code> (Chosen) and clear distance $\\ClearDistance$ = <code>[OilFilm.d_clear_str]</code> (Illustrative)</li>\n<li>Known-component budget, the seven rows of @tbl-enforcement-stopping-budget, which leave out the dispatch-to-admission interval $\\DispatchAdmissionInterval$ that is allocated but not yet measured: pre-brake delay $\\PrebrakeDelay$ = <code>[OilFilm.tau_delay_str]</code>, jerk bound $\\JerkLimit$ = <code>[OilFilm.j_brake_str]</code>, and fixed overheads of <code>[OilFilm.overhead_str]</code> (all Derived)</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Math</strong>:</p>\n<ol class=\"cs-list\">\n<li><strong>Friction floor</strong> (@eq-frontier-credible-deceleration): The brake binds only while $\\CoulombFrictionCoefficient \\ge \\CredibleBrakingDeceleration/\\Gravity$ = <code>[OilFilm.a_brake_str]</code> $/$ <code>[OilFilm.g_str]</code> = <code>[OilFilm.mu_min_str]</code>. Dry concrete clears it; the film does not, so $\\AvailableDeceleration = \\CoulombFrictionCoefficient \\Gravity$ = <code>[OilFilm.mu_oil_str]</code> $\\times$ <code>[OilFilm.g_str]</code> = <code>[OilFilm.a_oil_str]</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Dry-floor budget</strong>: Delay travel $\\SpeedAtDetection\\PrebrakeDelay$ $+$ braking $\\SpeedAtDetection^2/2\\AvailableDeceleration$ $+$ jerk-ramp travel $\\SpeedAtDetection \\AvailableDeceleration/2\\JerkLimit$ (the S-curve term of row 6, valid in the trapezoidal regime because $\\SpeedAtDetection &gt; \\AvailableDeceleration^2/\\JerkLimit$) $+$ overheads = <code>[OilFilm.v_tau_str]</code> $+$ <code>[OilFilm.brake_dry_str]</code> $+$ <code>[OilFilm.ramp_dry_str]</code> $+$ <code>[OilFilm.overhead_str]</code> = <code>[OilFilm.budget_aisle_str]</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Film budget</strong>: The same terms at $\\AvailableDeceleration$ = <code>[OilFilm.a_oil_str]</code> give <code>[OilFilm.v_tau_str]</code> $+$ <code>[OilFilm.brake_oil_str]</code> $+$ <code>[OilFilm.ramp_oil_str]</code> $+$ <code>[OilFilm.overhead_str]</code> = <code>[OilFilm.stop_oil_str]</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Result</strong>: On the film the stop grows from <code>[OilFilm.budget_aisle_str]</code> to <code>[OilFilm.stop_oil_str]</code>, far past the <code>[OilFilm.d_clear_str]</code> clear distance, before the dispatch-to-admission interval is even added.</p>\n<p><strong>Systems insight</strong>: Below $\\CoulombFrictionCoefficient$ = <code>[OilFilm.mu_min_str]</code> the floor, not the brake or the timing, sets the deceleration, and the braking term grows as $1/\\CoulombFrictionCoefficient$. Faster admission can recover only the <code>[OilFilm.v_tau_str]</code> of delay travel, so no improvement in the permission path restores the dry-floor mode on the film. Only a restriction or a measurement at the tire-floor interface can keep $\\StoppingDistance \\le \\ClearDistance$.</p>\n<p>: <strong>Amontons-Coulomb Friction</strong>: The classical laws of sliding friction make the friction force proportional to the normal load and independent of the apparent contact area, which is what lets a single coefficient $\\CoulombFrictionCoefficient$ describe a contact. @bowden1950friction traced the law to the small true area of contact, whose shear strength sets $\\CoulombFrictionCoefficient$, and showed that a lubricant film only molecules thick can lower it several-fold. The coefficient therefore belongs to the pair of surfaces and whatever lies between them, not to the floor alone, and the braking premise needs a measurement at the tire-floor interface that no camera viewing the floor supplies.</p>\n<p>The calculation omits the dispatch-to-admission interval, which the discrete placement of @sec-placement-dispatch-admission allocates but no loaded measurement yet confirms. That interval adds travel and reduces every derived speed ceiling, yet even the known-component budget already excludes the film. In fact, the friction premise did not appear only when fluid leaked onto the concrete. The candidate normal operating mode of @sec-enforcement-composite-budget always assumed that unmeasured friction floor across every aisle, dry or wet. Software timing and physical traction are independent requirements, since bounding scheduling delays does not guarantee floor grip and confirming floor grip does not remove scheduling delays. On the oil film, the drive electronics continue to regulate motor current and commanded torque within milliseconds, yet that torque slows the base only through tire-floor contact. Neither a faster enforcer nor another layer of neural inference therefore replaces the interface measurement or the speed restriction the notebook calls for.</p>\n<p>↩ Current regulation within milliseconds through electrical time constants and current clamping is established in @sec-body-electrical-time-constants.</p>\n<p>The manipulator arm's contact limit at the door latch has the same structure. Safety depends on a latch that holds under any force below the arm's contact ceiling. Mechanical wear, material fatigue, or corrosion can silently lower the latch's yield force below that ceiling, even while joint torque sensors report readings identical to those against a sound strike plate. A wrist force-torque sensor whose zero-point calibration has drifted creates the same hazard for every contact threshold that depends on its signal (@sec-frontier-passive-compliance-containment).</p>\n<p>In each case, a machine can leave its safe operating envelope while internal telemetry stays completely nominal. Successful test runs under nominal conditions provide no safety guarantee once an underlying physical premise fails. A safety case must explicitly list each unobserved premise, restrict operating authority while that premise remains unverified, and state the physical evidence required to close it. For every open entry, the engineer must determine what the machine can physically measure, what actuator limits can still prevent, and which operating claims must remain refused.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h3\">What the release claim rests on</h3>\n<p>Extensive testing of one link in a safety case cannot compensate for an unmeasured premise in another: a thousand hours of fault-free perception trials will not stop a base whose tires lose traction on an oily floor. A mobile manipulator's release claim forms a chain of dependent arguments tied to its operating hazards and chosen mitigations. The complete safety case must establish the operational design domain, measured actuator performance, stopping bounds, component interface contracts, and independent permission filtering for learned proposals. Hazards managed by runtime detection require verified detector coverage and bounded monitoring latency, whereas hazards managed through passive compliance or operating restrictions require physical evidence that those safeguards hold across declared payloads, component wear, and site conditions.</p>\n<p>: <strong>Popperian Falsification</strong>: The asymmetry holds for universal hypotheses [@popper1959logic]. No finite run of safe stops proves that every aisle floor supplies the friction floor, and one measured film below it refutes that premise. A rate claim, such as a hazard rate per hour, is neither refuted by one incident nor proved by many incident-free hours, so it is bounded statistically and revised as exposure accumulates. This chapter therefore holds deterministic premises in register entries and rate claims in confidence bounds.</p>\n<p>Each premise in a safety case carries one of five statuses: established by measurement or derivation, assumed, decided by accountable judgment, unsupported, or outside the threat model. (The Reader Guide lists the same five with examples; the audits of this chapter and its problems use these exact labels.) The envelope trichotomy (definition \\ref{dfn-release-envelope-trichotomy}) classifies an actively monitored runtime condition as known-true, known-false, or unknown. That trichotomy presupposes a monitor. \"Unknown\" marks a monitored condition whose evidence has lapsed, and the permission path answers it at once with a validated stop or hold until fresh evidence returns. An unobserved premise has no runtime monitor whose report can trigger that transition; its missing evidence must instead be recorded in the release argument. Telemetry stays nominal while the premise fails, and holding the machine until a report arrives would hold it forever, because no report will come. Such premises require standing restrictions decided before operation; a fallback cannot be triggered by evidence the machine never receives. Release certification must therefore either validate and maintain these premises through external physical evidence or restrict operating authority accordingly. For the mobile base, stopping-distance guarantees rest on an assumed wheel-floor friction coefficient that cannot be observed before braking. For the manipulator arm, contact safety assumes a minimum yield strength for the door latch and the objects it handles, a threshold set by engineering judgment rather than real-time measurement. For the wrist sensor, force-limiting safety functions assume zero-point calibration stability, a condition internal telemetry cannot verify without an unloaded physical tare. Recording these premises explicitly does not weaken a release case; it marks the exact boundary where system guarantees hold and beyond which operating authority must be withdrawn.</p>\n<p>Separating physical measurement from accountable judgment is essential to systems engineering. Testing and statistical analysis can estimate physical exposure, quantify sensor noise, and bound parameter uncertainty, but test data cannot choose an acceptable residual risk or decide which people in a facility must bear that risk. Those choices are accountable engineering decisions that must be recorded as explicit judgments rather than presented as empirical facts. Similarly, the verification campaign's threat model bounds where the safety record holds. Field trials across normal fleet workflows and random component faults do not by themselves support claims about deliberate attacks outside the tested conditions. A test campaign showing that a vision pipeline tolerates lens flare, sensor noise, and partial occlusions provides no evidence that it resists adversarial optical projections, tampered policy weights, or deliberate denial-of-service flooding on the internal communication bus.</p>\n<p>↩ The fault list that bounds what verification tests, including transduction-layer attacks on sensors and on the proposer, is established in @sec-verification-deriving-fault-list.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-4\" class=\"cs-h3\">Conditional shield credit and common-cause bounds</h3>\n<p>Within the threat model the safety case does cover, treating an independent permission path as a substitute for a missing floor-friction measurement asks the safety shield to stop a hazard it cannot sense. Even for hazards within its view, a shield reduces the failure rate only in proportion to its own tested coverage. Pricing what the permission path actually contributes to a hazard-rate claim separates the hazards a shield can attenuate from the register entries it can never close. @Sec-enforcement-minimal-intervention established deterministic guarantees for each command a safety shield checks, but turning those per-command guarantees into a system-level hazard rate requires statistical accounting. Because a shield acts as a conditional filter, the resulting hazard rate depends on how often the policy proposes hazardous actions, how reliably the shield intercepts them in those exact states, and how often failures occur outside the shield's sensing domain. Notebook \\ref{nbk-frontier-fleet-exposure-and-shielded-decomposition} prices these terms in an illustrative deployment.</p>\n<pre class=\"cs-code\"><code>#| echo: false\n#| warning: false\n#| message: false\nimport math\nfrom mlsysim import *\nfrom mlsysim.core.units import hour\nfrom mlsysim.core.units import day\nfrom mlsysim.fmt import fmt_sci, fmt, fmt_int, check\n\nclass ArchitecturalShieldDilution:\n    # ┌── 1. INPUT (Illustrative target and distinct evidence records) ────\n    _BILL = Scenarios.ShieldCoverageBill  # the fleet&#x27;s proposal log, the shield, and the target\n    p_target = _BILL.target_rate.to(1 / hour).magnitude  # category: illustrative (system incidents/hour target, not a statutory rate)\n    confidence = _BILL.confidence  # category: chosen (one-sided confidence level)\n    alpha = 1.0 - confidence  # category: derived (significance level)\n\n    fleet_units = _BILL.fleet_units  # category: illustrative (fleet size)\n    t_cycle_days = _BILL.cycle_days.to(day).magnitude  # category: illustrative (release cycle length)\n    hours_per_day = _BILL.hours_per_day.to(hour / day).magnitude  # category: illustrative (continuous operation)\n\n    c_shield_assumed = _BILL.coverage  # category: illustrative (class-specific interception coverage)\n    p_hw_assumed = _BILL.common_cause_rate.to(1 / hour).magnitude  # category: illustrative (hardware and common-cause residual set to zero)\n    proposal_events_hypothetical = _BILL.hazardous_proposals  # category: illustrative (adjudicated counterfactual-oracle proposal log)\n\n    # ┌── 2. COMPUTE (Proposal-log bound and shield decomposition) ────────\n    # The adjudicated proposal log covers one full cycle of the fleet; zero\n    # hazardous proposals bound the proposal rate (one-sided Poisson). The\n    # conditional design arithmetic gives the proposal-rate bound the target\n    # would require at the assumed coverage, and the exposure to show it.\n    bill = _BILL.result\n    proposal_exposure_hours_hypothetical = bill.exposure.to(hour).magnitude\n    p_proposal_upper_hypothetical = bill.proposal_rate_upper.to(1 / hour).magnitude\n    p_proposal_limit = bill.proposal_rate_limit.to(1 / hour).magnitude\n    n_brain_hours = bill.exposure_needed.to(hour)\n    t_test_days = bill.days_needed.to(day).magnitude\n    p_system_conditional = bill.residual_rate.to(1 / hour).magnitude\n    c_required_cycle = bill.required_coverage\n    test_overrun_days = bill.days_beyond_cycle.to(day).magnitude\n\n    # Coverage is itself a claim: zero misses in n seeded hazardous proposals of\n    # the class bound c_h from below. A joint statement from two one-sided bounds\n    # needs each at 1 - alpha/2 (Bonferroni), which moves both numbers.\n    n_coverage = bill.coverage_trials\n    confidence_split = bill.joint_confidence\n    n_coverage_joint = bill.coverage_trials_joint\n    p_proposal_upper_joint = bill.proposal_rate_upper_joint.to(1 / hour).magnitude\n    p_system_joint = bill.residual_rate_joint.to(1 / hour).magnitude\n\n    # ┌── 3. GUARD (Statistical bounds &amp; invariance checks) ──────────────\n    check(abs(proposal_exposure_hours_hypothetical - 216000.0) &lt; 1.0, f&quot;Unexpected proposal-log hours: {proposal_exposure_hours_hypothetical}&quot;)\n    check(abs(p_proposal_upper_hypothetical - 1.3869e-5) &lt; 1e-8, f&quot;Unexpected proposal bound: {p_proposal_upper_hypothetical}&quot;)\n    check(proposal_events_hypothetical == 0, &quot;Proposal scenario requires zero events&quot;)\n    check(abs(p_proposal_limit - 1.0e-5) &lt; 1e-9, f&quot;Unexpected proposal limit: {p_proposal_limit}&quot;)\n    check(abs(n_brain_hours.magnitude - 299573.0) &lt; 100.0, f&quot;Unexpected brain exposure: {n_brain_hours}&quot;)\n    check(abs(t_test_days - 124.8) &lt; 1.0, f&quot;Unexpected test days: {t_test_days}&quot;)\n    check(abs(p_system_conditional - 1.386913e-9) &lt; 1e-12, f&quot;Unexpected conditional residual: {p_system_conditional}&quot;)\n    check(abs(c_required_cycle - 0.9999278974) &lt; 1e-8, f&quot;Unexpected required coverage: {c_required_cycle}&quot;)\n    check(test_overrun_days &gt; 34.0, f&quot;Unexpected 90-day overrun: {test_overrun_days}&quot;)\n    check(n_coverage == 29956, f&quot;Coverage campaign at 95 percent should be 29,956 trials, got {n_coverage}&quot;)\n    check(n_coverage_joint == 36887, f&quot;Coverage campaign at 97.5 percent should be 36,887 trials, got {n_coverage_joint}&quot;)\n    check(abs(p_proposal_upper_joint - 1.7078e-5) &lt; 1e-8, f&quot;Unexpected 97.5 percent proposal bound: {p_proposal_upper_joint}&quot;)\n    check(abs(p_system_joint - 1.7078e-9) &lt; 1e-12, f&quot;Unexpected joint residual: {p_system_joint}&quot;)\n\n    # ┌── 4. OUTPUT (Canonical formatting) ────────────────────────────────\n    n_proposal_sci_str = fmt_sci(proposal_exposure_hours_hypothetical, precision=2)\n    p_proposal_upper_sci_str = fmt_sci(p_proposal_upper_hypothetical, precision=3)\n\n    p_brain_sci_str = fmt_sci(p_proposal_limit, precision=0)\n    n_brain_sci_str = fmt_sci(n_brain_hours.magnitude, precision=2)\n    t_test_days_str = fmt(t_test_days, precision=1)\n    p_system_conditional_sci_str = fmt_sci(p_system_conditional, precision=3)\n    c_required_str = fmt(c_required_cycle, precision=7)\n    test_overrun_days_str = fmt(test_overrun_days, precision=1)\n    n_coverage_str = fmt_int(n_coverage, commas=True)\n    n_coverage_joint_str = fmt_int(n_coverage_joint, commas=True)\n    p_proposal_upper_joint_sci_str = fmt_sci(p_proposal_upper_joint, precision=3)\n    p_system_joint_sci_str = fmt_sci(p_system_joint, precision=3)\n    # Operands shown in the notebook&#x27;s Math lines\n    fleet_units_str = fmt_int(fleet_units)\n    t_cycle_days_str = fmt(t_cycle_days, precision=0)\n    hours_per_day_str = fmt(hours_per_day, precision=0)\n    neg_ln_alpha_str = fmt(bill.ln_inv_alpha, precision=3)\n    neg_ln_alpha_half_str = fmt(bill.ln_inv_half_alpha, precision=3)\n    c_shield_str = fmt(c_shield_assumed, precision=4)\n    one_minus_c_sci_str = fmt_sci(1.0 - c_shield_assumed, precision=0)\n    p_target_sci_str = fmt_sci(p_target, precision=0)\n    confidence_pct_str = fmt_percent(confidence, precision=0, style=&quot;prose&quot;)\n    confidence_split_pct_str = fmt_percent(confidence_split, precision=1, style=&quot;prose&quot;)\n    alpha_str = fmt(alpha, precision=2)\n\np_target = ArchitecturalShieldDilution.p_target\n]```\n\nConsider an illustrative system target of `[ArchitecturalShieldDilution.p_target_sci_str]` $\\text{h}^{-1}$. This number comes from civil aviation certification, where catastrophic failure conditions must be shown to be &quot;extremely improbable,&quot; on the order of $10^{-9}$ per flight hour [@faa1988ac251309]. While no industrial robotics standard requires this threshold, examining it shows the statistical burden a borrowed safety target imposes. As the exposure wall established, physical operating hours alone cannot certify failure rates of this scale. The practical engineering question is what safety margin a runtime shield genuinely contributes against that target, and what supporting evidence its own operation requires.\n\n: **Extremely Improbable Band**: The advisory circular offers its probability bands as aids to engineering judgment, expressed per flight hour of a mean-duration flight and applied to each failure condition separately, and it grounds such criteria in historical accident data [@faa1988ac251309]. The $10^{-9}$ figure is therefore one catastrophic condition&#x27;s share of a certified design&#x27;s risk, not a rate for a whole aircraft, let alone for another kind of machine. Applying it elsewhere without the allocation argument that set its share leaves the number without its basis.\n\n: **Pooling Evidence Across Updates**: An updated model may alter the proposal distribution. Earlier data can be pooled only with a justified configuration-equivalence and exposure argument; an update does not automatically erase every observation. The exposure wall&#x27;s constant-rate model also requires a defensible treatment of shared fleet conditions and correlated faults.\n\n::: {.column-margin .margin-pointer}\n↩ The exposure wall, the event-free operating hours needed to bound a rare hazard rate, is established in @sec-evaluation-limits.\n:::\n\nA safety shield argument is inherently *conditional*. Observed incident rates across an operating fleet already include the protective interventions of runtime filters and human supervisors, so multiplying an observed incident rate by a shield&#x27;s interception factor double-counts that protection. For a defined hazard class $\\HazardClassIndex$, let $\\HazardousProposalRate$ denote the arrival rate of unshielded, hazardous action proposals emitted by the policy before the permission layer. Let $\\ShieldInterceptionCoverage = \\Pr(\\text{intercept} \\mid \\text{hazardous proposal}, \\HazardClassIndex)$ denote the verified interception coverage for that hazard class. Including an independently bounded hardware fault and common-cause arrival rate $\\HardwareCommonCauseRate$, the conditional hazard rate obeys @eq-frontier-shielded-hazard:\n$$\\SystemHazardRate \\le \\HazardousProposalRate(1-\\ShieldInterceptionCoverage)+\\HardwareCommonCauseRate$$ {#eq-frontier-shielded-hazard}\nHere, $\\SystemHazardRate$, $\\HazardousProposalRate$, and $\\HardwareCommonCauseRate$ are rates per operating hour, while $\\ShieldInterceptionCoverage$ is a unitless conditional probability. The limiting cases make the shield&#x27;s role plain. With perfect detection and interception ($\\ShieldInterceptionCoverage = 1$), the upper bound reduces to the hardware and common-cause term $\\HardwareCommonCauseRate$, a residual no coverage removes. Conversely, if the shield has zero coverage ($\\ShieldInterceptionCoverage = 0$), the raw proposal hazard rate passes straight to the actuators. This relation turns the runtime assurance architecture, which pairs an unverified primary controller with a verified safety filter [@sha2001using; @hsu2024safetyfilter], into a checkable hazard-rate claim.\n\n::: {.column-margin .margin-pointer}\n↩ The Simplex architecture, which pairs a capable unverified proposer with a verified protective controller, is established in @sec-enforcement-deterministic-gatekeeper.\n:::\n\nUsing @eq-frontier-shielded-hazard requires four premises. The first is a verified **counterfactual oracle**: an evaluation harness that determines whether an unshielded action proposal would have caused a safety violation. Without this oracle or another justified way to identify or conservatively bound hazardous proposals, the raw proposal error rate $\\HazardousProposalRate$ has no supporting evidence. The remaining requirements are timely hazard detection, adequate braking authority, and a careful accounting of correlated failures. Designers frequently overlook this final common-cause term. Release claim C5 required physical testing rather than a theoretical independence assumption because separate implementations of the same specification fail together far more often than statistical independence predicts (@sec-release-contextual-assumptions). The product $\\HazardousProposalRate(1-\\ShieldInterceptionCoverage)$ uses interception coverage conditional on hazardous proposals; substituting coverage measured on unrelated states erases that conditioning. An occluded camera frame that causes the neural policy to propose a bad trajectory can corrupt the safety shield&#x27;s state estimator at the same moment. That shared failure does not merely add an independent background term to $\\HardwareCommonCauseRate$; it lowers $\\ShieldInterceptionCoverage$ in the same states where policy errors happen. Interception coverage $\\ShieldInterceptionCoverage$ must therefore be measured across the actual states where the policy fails rather than on an idealized set of synthetic test cases. The equation gives no credit for hazards the shield cannot sense, such as oil films or fatigue-weakened latches, and no credit for the shield at all until each of the four premises is supported. Until then the release must restrict operation instead.\n\nBounding the system hazard rate requires bounding the raw proposal rate $\\HazardousProposalRate$ from an adjudicated execution log before crediting shield attenuation. Under a stationary Poisson arrival model with zero observed hazardous proposals, this upper bound follows the rule of three [@hanley1983nothing] from @eq-eval-rule-of-three, with proposal operating hours in place of discrete trials. For an adjudicated log spanning $\\Time$ operating hours at significance level $\\HypothesisTestSignificanceLevel$, the one-sided upper confidence bound is $-\\ln\\HypothesisTestSignificanceLevel/\\Time$, or roughly $3/\\Time$ at `[ArchitecturalShieldDilution.confidence_pct_str]` confidence. When the interception coverage $\\ShieldInterceptionCoverage$ is also estimated empirically from zero-miss seeded trials rather than given as a known constant, the product $\\HazardousProposalRate(1-\\ShieldInterceptionCoverage)$ rests on two separate confidence bounds that can each fail. Guaranteeing that both bounds hold simultaneously at overall significance $\\HypothesisTestSignificanceLevel$ requires splitting the error budget across the continuous proposal-log test and the discrete coverage-trial test via the union bound, testing each factor at significance $\\HypothesisTestSignificanceLevel/2$:\n\n::: {#nbk-frontier-fleet-exposure-and-shielded-decomposition .callout-notebook title=&quot;The coverage bill&quot;}\n\n**Problem**: In an illustrative scenario, a fleet keeps a fully adjudicated proposal log for one release cycle, judged by a validated counterfactual oracle, and the log records no hazardous proposal. If a named shield&#x27;s interception coverage for that hazard class were independently established, would the modeled residual of @eq-frontier-shielded-hazard meet the system target, and what evidence would it take to show that it does?\n\n**Variables**:\n\n- System target $\\TargetHazardRate$ = `[ArchitecturalShieldDilution.p_target_sci_str]` $\\text{h}^{-1}$ (Illustrative)\n- Proposal log $\\Time$ = `[ArchitecturalShieldDilution.fleet_units_str]` units $\\times$ `[ArchitecturalShieldDilution.t_cycle_days_str]` days $\\times$ `[ArchitecturalShieldDilution.hours_per_day_str]` h = `[ArchitecturalShieldDilution.n_proposal_sci_str]` proposal-hours with zero hazardous proposals (Illustrative)\n- Class-specific coverage $\\ShieldInterceptionCoverage$ = `[ArchitecturalShieldDilution.c_shield_str]` and $\\HardwareCommonCauseRate$ = 0 (Illustrative; the hardware and common-cause term is set to zero for this calculation)\n- Confidence `[ArchitecturalShieldDilution.confidence_pct_str]`, significance $\\HypothesisTestSignificanceLevel$ = `[ArchitecturalShieldDilution.alpha_str]` (Chosen)\n\n**Math**:\n\n1. **Proposal bound** (rule of three with hours in place of trials): $-\\ln\\HypothesisTestSignificanceLevel / \\Time$ = `[ArchitecturalShieldDilution.neg_ln_alpha_str]` $/$ `[ArchitecturalShieldDilution.n_proposal_sci_str]` h = `[ArchitecturalShieldDilution.p_proposal_upper_sci_str]` $\\text{h}^{-1}$.\n2. **Residual**: `[ArchitecturalShieldDilution.p_proposal_upper_sci_str]` $\\times$ `[ArchitecturalShieldDilution.one_minus_c_sci_str]` $+$ 0 = `[ArchitecturalShieldDilution.p_system_conditional_sci_str]` $\\text{h}^{-1}$, **above** the target. Meeting it at this bound needs $\\ShieldInterceptionCoverage \\ge 1 -$ `[ArchitecturalShieldDilution.p_target_sci_str]` $/$ `[ArchitecturalShieldDilution.p_proposal_upper_sci_str]` = `[ArchitecturalShieldDilution.c_required_str]`.\n3. **Proposal evidence at the assumed coverage**: The bound must fall to `[ArchitecturalShieldDilution.p_target_sci_str]` $/$ `[ArchitecturalShieldDilution.one_minus_c_sci_str]` = `[ArchitecturalShieldDilution.p_brain_sci_str]` $\\text{h}^{-1}$, which takes `[ArchitecturalShieldDilution.neg_ln_alpha_str]` $/$ `[ArchitecturalShieldDilution.p_brain_sci_str]` = `[ArchitecturalShieldDilution.n_brain_sci_str]` proposal-hours, or `[ArchitecturalShieldDilution.t_test_days_str]` days for the fleet, `[ArchitecturalShieldDilution.test_overrun_days_str]` days beyond the cycle.\n4. **Coverage evidence**: Bounding $\\ShieldInterceptionCoverage$ itself takes $\\ln$ `[ArchitecturalShieldDilution.alpha_str]` $/ \\ln$ `[ArchitecturalShieldDilution.c_shield_str]` = `[ArchitecturalShieldDilution.n_coverage_str]` seeded hazardous proposals of that class with no miss, drawn from the states in which the policy errs.\n5. **Joint confidence**: The probability that at least one of two bounds fails is at most the sum of their failure probabilities, so a joint `[ArchitecturalShieldDilution.confidence_pct_str]` statement needs each bound at `[ArchitecturalShieldDilution.confidence_split_pct_str]` (the Bonferroni split). The proposal bound becomes `[ArchitecturalShieldDilution.neg_ln_alpha_half_str]` $/$ `[ArchitecturalShieldDilution.n_proposal_sci_str]` h = `[ArchitecturalShieldDilution.p_proposal_upper_joint_sci_str]` $\\text{h}^{-1}$, the coverage campaign `[ArchitecturalShieldDilution.n_coverage_joint_str]` trials, and the residual `[ArchitecturalShieldDilution.p_system_joint_sci_str]` $\\text{h}^{-1}$.\n\n**Result**: The modeled residual misses the target even with the common-cause term set to zero. Supporting the claim takes both a proposal log longer than the release cycle and tens of thousands of seeded interceptions, and the confidence of the combined claim must be budgeted across them.\n\n**Systems insight**: A shield changes the system claim only for hazard classes it can detect and intercept in time. Proposal frequency, conditional interception coverage, hardware faults, and common-cause failures need separate evidence. Oil or material failure outside the shield&#x27;s sensing coverage stays in the residual-claims register.\n\n:::\n\nThe notebook prices what evidence the exposure model and the safety filter would need; it supplies none for either. Formal verification carries the same scoping requirement, since any mathematical proof applies only to the declared kinematic model, sensor observations, disturbance bounds, and timing guarantees. Whenever a physical premise lacks empirical proof, the release argument must substitute an operating restriction assigned to an accountable owner.\n\n::: {.column-margin .margin-pointer}\n↩ The scoping limits of formal certificates to their declared plant, observations, and disturbances are established in @sec-verification-formal-limits.\n:::\n\n### A residual-claims register {#sec-frontier-residual-claims-register}\n\nRefusing claim C3 in the safety case did not remove the floor-friction premise behind it. Without a durable record tying that open premise to an operating restriction, a later model update could restore normal aisle speed while the floor remains unmeasured. The **residual-claims register** is the sixteenth and closing record in the systems record chain. Taking in the upstream release manifest and approval record, the register binds every unresolved physical premise directly to an operating restriction.\n\n::: {.column-margin .margin-pointer}\n↪ The sixteen-record chain, which the residual-claims register closes, is developed in @sec-appendix-systems-record-register.\n:::\n\nEach register entry names four required elements: the missing physical observable, the active containment mechanism or operating restriction, the accountable owner, and a predeclared closure test. Together they give principle \\ref{pri-vol4-evidence-bounds-authority} direct operational force. Unlike a software defect log or issue tracker, the register is a machine-readable deployment record with a fixed schema. Its parameters set runtime enforcer bounds, and its standing conditions bind facility operations. Operating authority stays derated until test evidence passes the predeclared closure test. Writing a warning note in documentation, or deferring an open premise to a future release without lowering speed or narrowing the operational design domain, provides zero physical protection.\n\n::: {.column-margin .margin-pointer}\n↪ **Byte layout:** Wire formats, data fields, and structural invariants for register entries are documented in @sec-appendix-systems-wire-residual-claims.\n:::\n\nNot every open premise is physical. Entries that the approval record of @sec-responsibility-approval-record cannot close before release, such as a human role whose feasibility is still `UNKNOWN`, a decision waiting for an owner, or a group of people whose detection has not yet been measured, enter the register in the same form. Each carries the restriction that holds meanwhile, such as a lower speed wherever the unmeasured group works, an owner, and the test that would close it.\n\nEngineers must state each **missing observable** in concrete physical quantities rather than modeling jargon. Calling a failure an &quot;out-of-distribution latent state&quot; does not say what physical sensor must measure it or when an enforcer must intervene. Naming an unmeasured friction coefficient or latch yield force, by contrast, makes the engineering choices concrete. The team can weigh adding a sensor, adding a passive mechanical limit, or narrowing the operational design domain, and everyone can see what test evidence is needed to retire the restriction.\n\nThe undetected oil film forms the first register entry for the mobile manipulator. Its premise is that every aisle floor supplies a friction coefficient of at least $\\CoulombFrictionCoefficient \\ge$ `[OilFilm.mu_min_str]`, the floor needed for credible deceleration. The missing observable is the local friction coefficient under a clear fluid film, which neither onboard cameras nor lidars can tell apart from clean concrete. Because a stop sized for dry concrete overruns the available clearance on an oily floor, the normal speed limit is justified only on surfaces where friction is verified or actively controlled.\n\nAn open premise requires an immediate **operational response**. For warehouse aisles, release sign-off can authorize a derated speed of `[OilFilm.v_restricted_str]` on designated routes maintained under a facility inspection procedure. In this restricted-service mode, scheduled floor scrubbing, spill isolation, and controlled access must maintain a friction coefficient of at least $\\CoulombFrictionCoefficient \\ge$ `[OilFilm.mu_insp_str]`. That friction level gives an available deceleration of `[OilFilm.a_insp_str]` and a known-component speed ceiling of `[OilFilm.ceiling_insp_str]`. This is the inspected-floor ceiling that the release envelope carries (@tbl-16-odd-invariants), drawn as the dashed envelope in the stopping distance analysis of @sec-enforcement (@fig-12-stopping-vs-speed). The restricted mode sits below that ceiling rather than at it, because the ceiling, like the budget it comes from, still leaves out $\\DispatchAdmissionInterval$ and any reserve for it. This inspected level is an illustrative baseline chosen below dry concrete to tolerate light surface contamination. A production deployment must set this value from friction surveys across the designated routes and show how spill prevention or bounded cleanup maintains it between inspections. In the illustrative project records, restricted-mode operation remains provisional until the full timing budget, including dispatch-to-admission latency and the required reserves, is verified under load. Routes outside the facility inspection program are excluded from automated navigation.\n\nRestricted operation requires an accountable owner who can keep the underlying physical premise true. Facility operations owns this entry through its maintenance and spill-response procedures along pallet-jack routes, setting inspection intervals and specifying the site conditions that pause automated traffic. While the signed speed ceiling in the onboard permission path enforces the motion limit, a software speed setting alone cannot keep the floor dry.\n\nEvery register entry must also state a **closure test** before data collection begins. To detect surface films online, the closure test must qualify an independent physical sensor, since standard visual cameras and lidars cannot resolve clear fluids on concrete. Alternatively, higher-traction floor coatings or physical barriers separating leaky equipment could support a revised claim through facility evidence. One candidate closure campaign evaluates an optical film detector, requiring a miss rate of at most `[OilFilm.p_miss_target_pct_str]` demonstrated across `[OilFilm.n_closure_str]` zero-failure trials on seeded fluid films, the trial count required by the zero-failure sizing of @sec-evaluation-trials-per-claim. Until the sensor passes this test, the lower speed limit stays in force. Smaller test campaigns do not provide enough statistical confidence. Zero misses across `[OilFilm.n_contrast_str]` independent trials bounds the miss probability only at `[OilFilm.p_miss_contrast_str]` at `[OilFilm.confidence_pct_str]` confidence. In addition, the detector must see far enough ahead that sensing latency, permission processing, and braking finish before the wheels reach the fluid. Forward range, inference latency, false-alarm rate, and post-detection friction assumptions must all be declared in advance. Reactive wheel-slip reflexes can limit further acceleration once the wheels spin, but they cannot guarantee stopping distance before the tires hit the film.\n\nWhile the target miss rate of `[OilFilm.p_miss_target_pct_str]` is chosen illustratively here, a production deployment must derive it from the site&#x27;s hazard budget. The detector works inside the conditional shield relation of @eq-frontier-shielded-hazard, with fluid film encounters in place of hazardous action proposals. If films appear along transit routes at an arrival rate $\\FilmEncounterRate$ and facility engineering allocates a hazard budget $\\AllocatedHazardRate$ to this failure mode, the detector must satisfy $\\FilmEncounterRate\\,\\DetectorMissProbability \\le \\AllocatedHazardRate$. Choosing the risk allocation $\\AllocatedHazardRate$ is an accountable engineering judgment, whereas estimating the encounter rate $\\FilmEncounterRate$ comes from maintenance and spill logs.\n\nBeyond surface traction, aisle navigation adds a second register entry: moving people at blind corners. Stopping-distance budgets are sized against stationary obstacles, so a person who keeps walking toward the mobile base during the stop consumes clearance distance that the budget never set aside. As shown in @sec-enforcement-composite-budget, accounting for a person walking toward the robot throughout the stop caps the permissible base speed at the rack corner far below normal transit speeds. The safety premise behind normal aisle speed assumes that no person walks out of the occluded cross aisle during an emergency stop, leaving a person&#x27;s presence in the hidden cross aisle as an unobserved state. Restricted service therefore requires physical barriers or access controls that block human cross-traffic during automated runs; painted floor lines do not satisfy this condition. Facility operations owns this restriction. A proposed blind-corner presence sensor cannot earn credit in the safety case until its field of view, latency, and miss rate pass qualification under the same zero-failure statistical standard applied to the film detector.\n\n::: {.column-margin .margin-pointer}\n↩ Clearance eroded by a person approaching from an occluded cross aisle at walking speed is established in @sec-memory-belief-through-occlusion.\n:::\n\nThe automated picking station adds a third entry, covering what the arm grasps. Its premise is that the object the gripper picks up, and then hands to a coworker through the `accept_item` interface, is the object the task names. As shown in @sec-intent-failure-modes, semantic grounding can fail silently. When a thin-walled red glass jar sits alone where the red ceramic mug was expected, the grounding model mistakes the jar for the mug and still satisfies every geometric check in the planner and permission layers. The missing observables are object identity and crush strength, which neither wrist cameras nor visual foundation models can verify reliably before contact.\n\n::: {.column-margin .margin-pointer}\n↩ The `accept_item` handover entry, which grants no motion authority and caps the arm&#x27;s approach to the coworker&#x27;s hand, is established in @sec-intervention-capability-split.\n:::\n\nContaining this hazard takes a two-part operating restriction. First, facility operations restricts conveyor loading to verified items from a declared stock-keeping unit (SKU) list that excludes fragile look-alikes. Second, the pick station admits a grasp only when optical singulation confirms a single object in the pick window; multiple objects abort the automated intent and route the tote to manual picking. Singulation removes geometric confusion among permitted items, but it does not measure what an object is made of; an off-list glass jar arriving alone is kept out only by upstream loading controls. Lowering the grip-force limit enough to protect fragile items would require measured crush limits for every item in inventory, which is itself an unverified physical assumption. Facility operations and the application engineering team co-own the item catalog and singulation rules. The closure test requires qualifying an automated check at the station, such as a radio-frequency identification (RFID) reader or a combined scale-and-barcode check, with a false-accept rate of at most `[OilFilm.p_miss_target_pct_str]` at `[OilFilm.confidence_pct_str]` confidence across `[OilFilm.n_closure_str]` failure-free trials with seeded look-alikes. @Tbl-18-claims-register compares these three entries side by side, listing the four required components for each open premise.\n\n\\Needspace{31\\baselineskip}\n\n| Claim and missing state                                                                                                                                        | Hazard mechanism                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                              | Active containment and owner                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  | Predeclared closure test                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                     |\n|:---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|:--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|:------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|:-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| **Braking traction**: every aisle floor supplies $\\CoulombFrictionCoefficient \\ge$ `[OilFilm.mu_min_str]`; friction beneath a clear film is unobserved. | At $\\CoulombFrictionCoefficient$ = `[OilFilm.mu_oil_str]` the available deceleration falls to `[OilFilm.a_oil_str]`, and the known-component budget at `[OilFilm.v_aisle_str]` already grows to `[OilFilm.stop_oil_str]` against a `[OilFilm.d_clear_str]` clear distance. | Candidate in the actual records: `[OilFilm.v_restricted_str]` on qualified, controlled floors ($\\CoulombFrictionCoefficient \\ge$ `[OilFilm.mu_insp_str]`); full timing and reserve must support it. Uncontrolled routes excluded. *Owner*: site operations, through the spill procedure for pallet-jack routes. | A floor-film detector with a miss rate of at most `[OilFilm.p_miss_target_sym_str]` at `[OilFilm.confidence_sym_str]` confidence, from `[OilFilm.n_closure_str]` failure-free seeded-film trials, plus timing and clearance tests; the restriction holds until it passes.                               |\n| **Walking person**: no person approaches the rack end during a stop; presence in the hidden cross aisle is unobserved.                                         | A person walking at `[OilFilm.v_h_str]` through the whole stop would cap the base far below its aisle speed at the rack end (@sec-enforcement-composite-budget).                                                                                                                                       | Controlled separation excluding continuing human approach during a stop; no credit for an unqualified presence sensor. *Owner*: site operations.                                                                                                                                                                              | Qualify coverage, detection timing, and the complete response; `[OilFilm.n_closure_str]` failure-free trials support a `[OilFilm.p_miss_target_sym_str]` miss bound at `[OilFilm.confidence_sym_str]` confidence for the declared trial population.                                                     |\n| **Object identity**: the object grasped is the object the task names; identity and fragility before contact are unobserved.                                    | A confident grounding to a thin-walled glass jar in place of the absent mug passes every geometric check, and a grip the ceramic mug needs can crush the glass.                                                                                                                                               | Feed control admits to the conveyor only items on the station&#x27;s declared list, none of them a fragile look-alike; singulation admits a grasp only with one object in the pick window. An off-list item arriving alone is contained only by the feed procedure. *Owner*: site operations, jointly with the task profile.       | A qualified identity check at the station (tag read or scale-and-code) with a false-accept rate of at most `[OilFilm.p_miss_target_sym_str]` at `[OilFilm.confidence_sym_str]` confidence, from `[OilFilm.n_closure_str]` failure-free seeded look-alike trials; the restriction holds until it passes. |\n\n: **Residual-claims register for the aisle and the pick station**: Proposed responses to three open premises behind the mobile manipulator&#x27;s aisle speed and its pick; operating credit requires the supporting evidence stipulated in the restricted-service branch. For each, the register names the missing physical observable, the hazard it permits, the operating restriction and its accountable owner, and the predeclared closure test that would retire the restriction. {#tbl-18-claims-register tbl-colwidths=&quot;[22,24,26,28]&quot;}\n\nThe register also holds a fourth entry, a timing premise, which the table leaves out because its hazard is the same overrun as the first row&#x27;s and only its missing evidence differs. Measured capture-to-dispatch latency excludes the downstream dispatch-to-admission interval. The placement allocation budgets that interval at `[OilFilm.delta_da_str]` out of the `[OilFilm.t_latest_str]` timing allowance at the aisle speed, leaving `[OilFilm.reserve_da_str]` of reserve. Stated in the register&#x27;s four elements, the missing observable is the loaded worst-case dispatch-to-admission interval, which steady-state inference benchmarks do not bound. Because that interval remains unverified, the runtime systems team owns an active restriction that holds the aisle speed as an uncertified candidate rather than an authorized mode. The closure test is supported timing and response analysis under declared workload and resource bounds, challenged by loaded and adversarial measurements of both latency intervals. Until that test shows that the interval fits its allocation with positive reserve and meets the lease duration and renewal requirements of the execution contracts, the restriction stays in force. The stipulated service branch assumes that timing evidence for its derated mode; in the baseline records the entry remains open.\n\n#### Maintaining the restrictions {#sec-frontier-operationalizing-claims}\n\nWriting an open assumption into a machine-readable register file does not turn it into a measured physical state. If the signed speed limits and site procedures are not wired into the release gate and daily floor work, the restriction exists only on paper. Operating restrictions in the register must therefore attach directly to the runtime enforcers and site workflows that govern daily operation. Cryptographically signed speed envelopes constrain the onboard permission path, while scheduled maintenance intervals bind facility operations. Release pipelines check that both controls are active in the authorized configuration, while runtime monitors enforce only the physical bounds that onboard sensors can read.\n\nModel retraining or software updates must inherit every open restriction whose closure test has not yet passed. Release gates must check the signed operating limits and the status of their supporting evidence before authorizing a deployment. Dropping to a restricted mode requires its own validated safety case; an open register entry does not automatically clear a slower mode for service. At runtime, low-level controllers enforce speed ceilings and actuator limits, while facility crews maintain floor traction and conveyor loading rules at the scheduled intervals.\n\nA restriction can also lapse in the middle of a motion, when a signed envelope expires, an inspection interval runs out while the base is in a route, or a deployment gate withdraws a mode. The lapse must hand over to the fallback ladder rather than to a generic stop. A trajectory chunk already executing inside the old restriction may finish only if its lease, state estimate, envelope, and supporting physical premises remain valid through completion. Withdrawing permission for new proposals can preserve an already admitted chunk under those conditions; loss of a premise that supports its execution cannot. Losing active permission altogether demands a physically feasible stop within the remaining budget. The rung must match the state, because controlled deceleration, holding brakes at zero velocity, torque removal, and full power isolation produce different transients. No register entry can replace those physical distinctions with a single emergency-stop primitive.\n\n::: {.column-margin .margin-pointer}\n↩ The ladder of state-matched fallback responses, from minimal projection to complete torque removal, is established in @sec-enforcement-fallback-ladder.\n:::\n\nNew sensors can justify wider operating envelopes only after they pass empirical qualification. Where real-time detection is impractical, the register must rely on a conservative action, passive containment, periodic proof testing, or a restricted operating domain (the responses to a hidden state that @sec-frontier-passive-compliance-containment develops). Choosing among those responses requires comparing the distance at which a detector could spot the hazard against the deadline for committed motion.\n\n### Failures without detectors {#sec-frontier-failures-without-detectors}\n\nTo see how that distance-versus-deadline comparison works on the first register entry, an automated floor-film detector can retire the surface-friction restriction only if its coverage, false-alarm rate, and detection latency fit inside the route&#x27;s stopping budget rather than a standalone model benchmark. In the known-component budget, running at the candidate aisle speed leaves `[OilFilm.spare_aisle_str]` of physical margin, which corresponds to an allowable delay of `[OilFilm.t_latest_str]`. Setting aside `[OilFilm.delta_da_str]` for dispatch-to-admission processing leaves `[OilFilm.reserve_da_str]` of timing reserve. That reserve is an upper bound on unallocated latency, not a certified budget for new perception pipelines, and it offers no protection against a pedestrian who keeps walking toward the robot during the stop.\n\nA forward-looking fluid detector faces an even tighter spatial deadline. The required detection range, minus the distance the robot travels during sensor integration and neural inference, must cover the full dry-floor stopping distance before the tires reach the leading edge of the film:\n$$\\RequiredDetectionRange \\ge \\SpeedAtDetection\\,\\DetectionInterval + \\KnownComponentStopDistance(\\SpeedAtDetection),$$ {#eq-frontier-film-detection-range}\nwhere $\\KnownComponentStopDistance(\\SpeedAtDetection)$ denotes the known-component dry-floor budget of notebook \\ref{nbk-frontier-friction-floor}, the seven rows of @tbl-enforcement-stopping-budget. The detection interval $\\DetectionInterval$ covers only the film detector&#x27;s own work, from the moment the film enters its range to the moment it reports. That report is the observation that starts the pre-brake chain, so the observation age and admission already inside $\\KnownComponentStopDistance$ follow it rather than overlap it. Where the two do overlap in a real pipeline, adding them is conservative. With a chosen sensing and inference latency of `[OilFilm.t_detect_str]` at the `[OilFilm.v_aisle_str]` aisle speed, the detector must see the film at least `[OilFilm.d_detect_min_str]` ahead. That range is almost exactly the `[OilFilm.d_clear_str]` clear distance, and not by coincidence. The dry budget and the spare together make up the clear distance, and the detector&#x27;s `[OilFilm.t_detect_str]` of travel nearly equals the `[OilFilm.t_latest_str]` that the spare buys. A detector that sees only as far as the clear distance therefore spends the whole timing allowance by itself, leaving nothing for the `[OilFilm.delta_da_str]` dispatch-to-admission allocation. To fit beside that allocation, its latency must stay under the `[OilFilm.reserve_da_str]` reserve, or its range must grow. If classification occurs only after the drive wheels enter the fluid, the stopping distance expands to `[OilFilm.stop_oil_str]`, far beyond the `[OilFilm.d_clear_str]` clear distance. A classification received after the wheels cross onto the film can serve incident analysis, but it arrives too late to avert the collision.\n\n#### Observational indistinguishability and the action deadline {#sec-frontier-observational-indistinguishability-sub}\n\nWhen an oil film and dry concrete produce identical camera and lidar readings until the robot is already inside its dry-floor stopping distance, no classifier running on those sensors can trigger a stop in time. That limit is observational indistinguishability. Consider two physical world states, $\\SafeWorldState$ and $\\HazardousWorldState$. In state $\\SafeWorldState$, a commanded actuator trajectory $\\ControlInput(\\TimeInstant)$ runs safely. In state $\\HazardousWorldState$, that same command $\\ControlInput(\\TimeInstant)$ causes structural damage or human injury, requiring the permission path to trigger an emergency stop. The constraint governing this stop is distance rather than elapsed time: because braking changes the robot&#x27;s speed along its path, the point of harm is a place in the room. Let $\\RemainingHarmDistance$ denote the remaining distance to that boundary when the distinguishing observation arrives. An emergency stop finishes short of the hazard only if:\n$$\\RemainingHarmDistance \\ge \\SpeedAtDetection\\,(\\EnforcerComputeTime + \\ActuatorResponseTime) + \\BrakingDistance(\\SpeedAtDetection),$$ {#eq-frontier-action-deadline}\nmatching the physical stopping relation used throughout this book. The enforcer&#x27;s compute time and the actuator&#x27;s response time together act as the pre-brake delay $\\PrebrakeDelay$, counted from the arrival of the distinguishing observation rather than from a fixed capture event, and $\\BrakingDistance(\\SpeedAtDetection)$ is the braking distance of the chosen stop. Dividing this distance constraint by the approach speed gives an equivalent observation deadline: $\\DeadlineInstant = \\HarmInstant - \\EnforcerComputeTime - \\ActuatorResponseTime - \\BrakingDistance(\\SpeedAtDetection)/\\SpeedAtDetection$, where $\\HarmInstant$ is the instant the unbraked trajectory would reach impact. Because $\\HarmInstant$ marks when the robot would reach the hazard at constant cruising speed $\\SpeedAtDetection$, the quantity $\\HarmInstant - \\DeadlineInstant$ expresses the required spatial clearance $\\RemainingHarmDistance$ in units of cruising-speed travel time. Once braking begins, the decelerating robot takes longer than $\\BrakingDistance(\\SpeedAtDetection)/\\SpeedAtDetection$ to cover the remaining distance, so it safely comes to rest just short of the spatial boundary at a clock instant later than $\\HarmInstant$. A common mistake subtracts the full stopping duration $\\StoppingDuration$ instead of the speed-normalized distance $\\BrakingDistance/\\SpeedAtDetection$, treating the robot as if it kept moving at full cruising speed until the instant it came to rest. For the jerk-limited profile from `[OilFilm.v_aisle_str]`, braking lasts `[OilFilm.t_brake_aisle_str]` but covers a distance equal to only `[OilFilm.t_brake_equiv_str]` of constant-speed travel. Writing the deadline purely in time over-reserves `[OilFilm.t_overreserve_str]`, or `[OilFilm.d_overreserve_str]` of clearance, an error larger than the entire `[OilFilm.spare_aisle_str]` spare. The time form errs on the safe side, but it refuses operating modes that the distance form rightly admits. Manipulator contact follows the same logic: once an end-effector touches a stiff surface, contact force rather than position sets the observation deadline (@sec-enforcement-intentional-contact).\n\n\n\nTo visualize how that observation deadline works across both motion and contact, the paired worlds in @fig-18-indistinguishable-deadline separate physical state from observable history. Their common brake command has different consequences, although the depicted channel supplies the same inputs through the action deadline.\n\n::: {#fig-18-indistinguishable-deadline fig-env=&quot;figure&quot; fig-pos=&quot;htb&quot; fig-cap=&quot;**Different Worlds, Identical Timely Evidence**: The dry-floor and hidden-film cases share an approach and brake command but reach different stopping positions. Their schematic observation traces remain identical until after the deadline set by @eq-frontier-action-deadline. A later distinction cannot justify a state-specific response that had to begin earlier. The traces illustrate the stated observation policy; they do not prove that every admissible probe or additional sensor would also fail.&quot; fig-alt=&quot;Two wheeled machines approach marked harm boundaries. One brake path stops short and the other crosses the boundary above a dashed floor patch. Below, matching dry and film traces diverge after a vertical action-deadline line.&quot;}\n\n:::\n\nA timely separating observation changes this argument only if the machine can obtain it within the same physical budget. When no such observation is available, the permission path must choose an action qualified for both candidate states or withhold the operating mode.\n\nIf the sensor stream $\\MeasurementVector(\\TimeInstant)$ produced under state $\\SafeWorldState$ matches that under $\\HazardousWorldState$ up to $\\DeadlineInstant$ for every admissible control input, no algorithm acting on those measurements can tell the safe state apart from the hazardous state in time to prevent harm:\n\n::: {#dfn-frontier-observational-indistinguishability .callout-definition title=&quot;Observational indistinguishability&quot;}\n\n***Observational indistinguishability*** is the condition in which two physical states that require different responses produce the same observation history, under every input the machine may admissibly apply, until the deadline by which a state-specific response must begin.\n\n1.  **Significance**: It turns an observation limit into a stopping-budget violation. The deadline is set by @eq-frontier-action-deadline, so if the film and the dry floor look identical until the remaining distance falls below $\\SpeedAtDetection\\,(\\EnforcerComputeTime + \\ActuatorResponseTime) + \\BrakingDistance(\\SpeedAtDetection)$, that evidence cannot justify continuing the dry-floor mode. The machine must instead use a response already qualified for both states or withhold that mode.\n2.  **Distinction**: Noise and weak detectors obscure information that more data or a better model can recover; indistinguishability means the information is absent from the channel. Unlike unobservability (definition \\ref{dfn-memory-observability}), which asks whether a state can ever be reconstructed from the output history, it is tied to a deadline and to the inputs the machine may apply.\n3.  **Common pitfall**: A frequent misconception is that a more capable classifier on the same channel will eventually separate the states. Identical histories give every classifier identical inputs. A designed probe can separate states hidden under the current observation policy only when an admissible excitation reveals a difference before the deadline. If every admissible input leaves the histories identical, the response requires a new channel or an action valid in both states.\n\n:::\n\n::: {.column-margin .margin-pointer}\n↩ The observation contract, which bounds the age and error of what a channel reports but cannot certify a state the channel never carries, is established in @sec-perception-observation-contract.\n:::\n\nThe qualification *under every input the machine may admissibly apply* matters because whether two states look different often depends on how the machine excites the plant, as shown in nonlinear observability theory [@hermann1977nonlinear]. In some settings, an engineer can inject a small, safe control test input that makes the two states produce different sensor readings before the deadline. This technique is **active probing**: applying a bounded test excitation to expose hidden state differences in the sensor stream before the action deadline, the same excitation that @sec-memory-state-estimation-bounds uses to expose a drifting force-sensor bias. Active probing draws on auxiliary signal design from fault detection [@campbell2004auxiliary]. For example, a controlled tug at a qualified proof load can check whether a door latch holds at that load before the arm commits to its full pull, though it does not measure the latch&#x27;s remaining yield margin. On the floor, a brief torque pulse at low speed, applied before the base enters a route, makes the wheels slip measurably on a film and not on dry concrete, turning the friction under the tires into telemetry. Such a slip check measures only the patch of floor the wheels already stand on; it cannot see a film ahead, which is why the forward detector analyzed earlier has its own range condition. Active probing costs time and disturbs the motion, and both costs must fit inside the safety budgets. Where no admissible probe can separate the states within the timing budget, the design must fall back on conservative actions or passive mechanical containment.\n\n: **Auxiliary Signal Design**: The multimodel method computes a small test input that guarantees the normal model and each declared failure model produce different outputs within a fixed test horizon, despite bounded disturbances [@campbell2004auxiliary]. A probe can therefore rule out only the failures someone wrote down as models, which is why the hidden premise must first be named in the register. The input bound and the horizon map onto this chapter&#x27;s budgets, since the probe must fit the force envelope and the separation must arrive before $\\DeadlineInstant$.\n\n#### Responses when the state remains hidden {#sec-frontier-passive-compliance-containment}\n\nActive probing is the first of five design responses when passive runtime observations leave candidate physical states indistinguishable. When an admissible excitation can separate the states before the deadline, *active probing* supplies the missing observation online; if every admissible input leaves them indistinguishable, the remaining four responses must carry the claim without online separation. *Proof testing* measures the state from outside the runtime channel at scheduled intervals, such as an unloaded tare of a sensor or a load test of a latch, and covers the time between tests by argument. A *conservative action* chooses a command safe in both candidate states, such as a speed low enough for the film. When control commands alone cannot bound the interaction, *passive containment* limits the physical consequence mechanically, such as a compliant element that caps contact force whatever the software believes. A *restricted operational domain* withholds authority altogether from states that cannot be told apart, such as routes without a spill procedure. The preceding register entries already used the conservative action and the restricted domain, and active probing was shown at the latch and the floor. The following wrist-sensor and latch examples show proof testing and passive containment at work on the running machine.\n\nSensor drift is the slow form of the same barrier. The wrist strain gauge of @sec-memory-state-estimation-bounds, whose bias wanders as thermal cycles and mechanical stress relaxation alter its bridge resistance, reports only the sum of contact force and bias. A drifting bias and a real change in force therefore produce the same reading and a zero innovation. What this chapter adds is the choice of response. Every contact threshold that depends on that signal inherits the drift, and no response chosen at runtime can see it. The response must therefore be proof testing, meaning an unloaded tare or a proof-test fixture at an interval short enough that the drift accumulated between tests stays inside the threshold&#x27;s margin.\n\n::: {.column-margin .margin-pointer}\n↩ Mechanical degradation and thermal calibration drift as continuing obligations of a release verdict are established in @sec-release-calibration-drift.\n:::\n\nA weakened door latch poses the same observability barrier on a millisecond timescale. Before the metal yields, joint torque sensors read the same reaction force against a fatigued latch as against a sound one, so the torque channel carries no information about how much yield margin remains. Because plastic yield is permanent, no software command sent after the latch bends can undo the damage, and running a faster classifier on joint torque signals adds zero protection. A credible safety strategy must bring in information from outside the runtime torque channel. Scheduled proof tests check that the latch survives a specified proof load at fixed intervals, without measuring its ultimate yield strength. Even so, proof testing holds only if maintenance records show that fatigue, wear, and corrosion cannot push the latch&#x27;s yield strength below the arm&#x27;s contact ceiling between tests.\n\n: **Proof Test**: Functional safety defines a proof test as a periodic test that reveals dangerous failures no online diagnostic can detect, so that the function can be restored to its as-new condition [@iec61508]. A hidden failure that arrives at a constant rate $\\ComponentFailureRate$ is present, on average over a test interval $\\Time$, for a fraction of about $\\ComponentFailureRate \\Time/2$ of the time when $\\ComponentFailureRate \\Time$ is small. The interval is therefore itself a safety parameter, because it bounds how long a weakened latch can go unseen.\n</code></pre>\n<p>#| echo: false #| warning: false #| message: false</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ A SERIES COMPLIANT ELEMENT AT THE DOOR LATCH</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Context: @sec-frontier-passive-compliance-containment,</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          @eq-frontier-passive-compliance.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Goal:    Size the stiffness and stroke of a series spring that lets the</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          arm meet the latch at the faster approach speed without passing</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          the declared force floor, using the registry's latch values.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Show:    rigid-plate result at both approach speeds; the stiffness bound;</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          the element stiffness, stroke, and the peak if torque is removed.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ How:     after the tripwire, the arm travels v t_resp + v^2/(2 a_arm)</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          (calc_latch_contact_stopping_profile, the Ch10 model); the force</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          condition F_trip + k_eq * stroke &lt;= F_floor bounds the series</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          stiffness k_eq; springs in series give the element stiffness; a</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          coasting mass stores m v^2/2 on top of the tripwire energy.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>import math from mlsysim import * from mlsysim.core.units import kg, kilonewton, meter, millimeter, ms, newton, second</p>\n<p>RM = Embodied.MobileManipulator.WarehouseMobileManipulator AISLE = Embodied.Scenario.WarehouseAisle</p>\n<p>class LatchCompliance:</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 1. LOAD (Registry and chosen design inputs) ──────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>k_plate = AISLE.k_latch  # category: illustrative (strike-plate contact stiffness) v_guarded = AISLE.v_latch_approach  # category: illustrative (guarded approach, the declared ODD) v_fast = AISLE.v_latch_high  # category: illustrative (faster approach the policy proposes) f_trip = AISLE.f_latch_tripwire  # category: chosen (contact tripwire) t_resp = AISLE.t_contact_response  # category: illustrative (tripwire to brake onset) f_floor = AISLE.f_latch_limit  # category: illustrative (declared force floor, a judgment, not a measured strength) m_eff = RM.arm_effective_contact_mass  # category: derived (robot-side effective mass at the TCP) _SPRING = Scenarios.WarehouseLatchSeriesSpring  # the spring sized for the fast approach f_pre = _SPRING.preload  # category: chosen (series spring without preload)</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 2. EXECUTE (Compute) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>a_arm = _SPRING.arm_deceleration  # hold the arm to the guarded approach's stop a_arm_fast_rigid = _SPRING.arm_deceleration_rigid_fast</p>\n<p>stroke_fast = _SPRING.driven_stroke  # driven stroke after the tripwire k_eq_max = _SPRING.series_stiffness_max k_spring_max = _SPRING.spring_stiffness_max stroke_needed = _SPRING.spring_stroke  # element deflection at the floor force</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">Peak force if drive torque is removed at the tripwire: f0^2/(2k) + m v^2/2 = F^2/(2k)</h1>\n<p>f_coast_rigid_guarded = _SPRING.coasting_rigid_guarded f_coast_rigid_fast = _SPRING.coasting_rigid_fast f_coast_compliant_fast = _SPRING.coasting_spring_fast check_profile = _SPRING.spring_fast_profile</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 3. GUARD (Invariants) ────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>check(abs(a_arm.magnitude - 2.9508) &lt; 5e-4, f\"Guarded-approach arm stop should be 2.95 m/s^2, got {a_arm}.\") check(abs(a_arm_fast_rigid.magnitude - 400.0) &lt; 1e-6, f\"Rigid plate at the fast approach needs 400 m/s^2, got {a_arm_fast_rigid}.\") check(abs(stroke_fast.magnitude - 1.8944) &lt; 5e-4, f\"Driven stroke at the fast approach should be 1.89 mm, got {stroke_fast}.\") check(abs(k_eq_max.magnitude - 44868.0) &lt; 5.0, f\"Series stiffness bound should be 44.9 kN/m, got {k_eq_max}.\") check(abs(k_spring_max.magnitude - 50537.0) &lt; 5.0, f\"Element stiffness should be 50.5 kN/m, got {k_spring_max}.\") check(abs(stroke_needed.magnitude - 1.979) &lt; 5e-3, f\"Element stroke should be 1.98 mm, got {stroke_needed}.\") check(k_eq_max &lt; k_plate, \"The fast approach needs a softer load path than the rigid plate.\") check(abs(check_profile[\"a_arm_needed\"].magnitude - a_arm.magnitude) &lt; 1e-6, \"With k_eq at the bound, the Ch10 model needs the same arm stop.\") check(f_coast_rigid_guarded &lt; f_floor, \"At the guarded approach the rigid plate stays under the floor even if torque is removed.\") check(f_coast_rigid_fast &gt; 2 * f_floor, \"At the fast approach the rigid plate more than doubles the floor.\") check(f_coast_compliant_fast &lt; f_floor, \"With the element, removing torque at the tripwire stays under the floor.\")</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">margin_latch_coasting_peak_envelope.svg fig-alt hand-types 67, 220, 75 and 100 N, 0.03 and 0.1 m/s</h1>\n<p>check(abs(f_coast_rigid_guarded.magnitude - 67.4) &lt; 0.05, f\"Rigid guarded coasting peak should be 67.4 N, got {f_coast_rigid_guarded}.\") check(abs(f_coast_rigid_fast.magnitude - 219.6) &lt; 0.05, f\"Rigid fast coasting peak should be 219.6 N, got {f_coast_rigid_fast}.\") check(abs(f_coast_compliant_fast.magnitude - 74.9) &lt; 0.05, f\"Compliant fast coasting peak should be 74.9 N, got {f_coast_compliant_fast}.\") check(abs(f_floor.magnitude - 100.0) &lt; 1e-9, f\"Declared force floor should be 100 N, got {f_floor}.\") check( abs(v_guarded.to(meter / second).magnitude - 0.03) &lt; 1e-9 and abs(v_fast.to(meter / second).magnitude - 0.1) &lt; 1e-9, \"Approach speeds should be 0.03 and 0.1 m/s.\", )</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 4. OUTPUT (Formatting) ───────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>k_plate_str = fmt_sci_qty(k_plate, newton / meter, precision=1) v_guarded_str = fmt_velocity(v_guarded, precision=2) v_fast_str = fmt_velocity(v_fast, precision=2) f_trip_str = fmt_force(f_trip, precision=0) t_resp_str = fmt_latency(t_resp, unit=ms, precision=0) f_floor_str = fmt_force(f_floor, precision=0) m_eff_str = fmt_mass(m_eff, unit=kg, precision=0) a_arm_str = fmt_acceleration(a_arm, precision=2) a_arm_fast_rigid_str = fmt_acceleration(a_arm_fast_rigid, precision=0) stroke_fast_str = fmt_length(stroke_fast, unit=millimeter, precision=2) k_eq_max_str = fmt_qty(k_eq_max.to(kilonewton / meter), unit=kilonewton / meter, precision=1) k_spring_max_str = fmt_qty(k_spring_max.to(kilonewton / meter), unit=kilonewton / meter, precision=1) stroke_needed_str = fmt_length(stroke_needed, unit=millimeter, precision=1) f_coast_rigid_guarded_str = fmt_force(f_coast_rigid_guarded, precision=1) f_coast_rigid_fast_str = fmt_force(f_coast_rigid_fast, precision=1) f_coast_compliant_fast_str = fmt_force(f_coast_compliant_fast, precision=1) ]```</p>\n<p>Adding a passive compliant element to the structure can mechanically cap interaction forces across a qualified deflection stroke, the principle behind series elastic actuators [@pratt1995series] and mechanical torque limiters. Two physical conditions govern compliant load paths, both set by the stroke the arm <em>actively drives</em> after contact detection rather than the total uncompressed spring length. When a contact tripwire $\\ContactTripwireForce$ triggers, the arm keeps moving during its controller response latency $\\ContactResponseLatency$ before decelerating at rate $\\ArmStoppingDeceleration$, driving the mechanism through a post-contact stroke of $\\PostContactDrivenStroke = \\SpeedAtDetection\\,\\ContactResponseLatency + \\SpeedAtDetection^2/(2\\ArmStoppingDeceleration)$. To keep the contact force below the declared strength floor $\\LatchStrengthFloor$ (a lower bound on the latch's strength, unrelated to the floor friction), the equivalent stiffness $\\EquivalentSeriesStiffness$ of the combined series load path (compliant element and strike plate) must satisfy @eq-frontier-passive-compliance: $$\\ContactTripwireForce + \\EquivalentSeriesStiffness\\,\\PostContactDrivenStroke \\le \\LatchStrengthFloor \\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad \\EquivalentSeriesStiffness \\le \\frac{\\LatchStrengthFloor-\\ContactTripwireForce}{\\SpeedAtDetection\\,\\ContactResponseLatency + \\SpeedAtDetection^2/(2\\ArmStoppingDeceleration)}.$$ {#eq-frontier-passive-compliance} In addition, the compliant element's allowable stroke must exceed its deflection under the peak allowable force, $(\\LatchStrengthFloor-\\CompliantElementPreload)/\\CompliantElementStiffness$, so the spring never bottoms out against a hard stop. The threshold $\\LatchStrengthFloor$ is not the latch's actual yield strength, which remains unobserved between proof tests; it is a chosen lower strength bound recorded as an assumption in the register until physical evidence supports it. Accountable judgment chooses the design bound; it cannot create material strength.</p>\n<p>The warehouse door latch shows what passive compliance buys. During a guarded approach at <code>[LatchCompliance.v_guarded_str]</code>, meeting a stiff strike plate of stiffness <code>[LatchCompliance.k_plate_str]</code> satisfies @eq-frontier-passive-compliance with a required arm deceleration of <code>[LatchCompliance.a_arm_str]</code>, matching the guarded latch stop derived in @sec-enforcement-intentional-contact. At a faster approach speed of <code>[LatchCompliance.v_fast_str]</code>, however, the same stiff contact would require an impossible arm deceleration of <code>[LatchCompliance.a_arm_fast_rigid_str]</code>, forcing the permission path to refuse the trajectory. Holding arm deceleration to the guarded stopping bound instead drives the arm through <code>[LatchCompliance.stroke_fast_str]</code> after it crosses the <code>[LatchCompliance.f_trip_str]</code> tripwire, capping the allowable series stiffness at <code>[LatchCompliance.k_eq_max_str]</code>. Because series compliance combines inversely ($1/\\EquivalentSeriesStiffness = 1/\\CompliantElementStiffness + 1/\\LatchPlateStiffness$), pairing the compliant element with the strike plate requires a spring stiffness of at most <code>[LatchCompliance.k_spring_max_str]</code> and an available stroke of at least <code>[LatchCompliance.stroke_needed_str]</code> to stay within the <code>[LatchCompliance.f_floor_str]</code> design force.</p>\n<p>This kinematic calculation assumes a stiff position-tracking drive that enforces the commanded $\\ArmStoppingDeceleration$ deceleration profile throughout the post-tripwire stroke regardless of contact reaction. Once spring compression exceeds $\\EffectiveImpactMass\\,\\ArmStoppingDeceleration$, the passive spring alone would decelerate the $\\EffectiveImpactMass$ effective mass faster than $\\ArmStoppingDeceleration$, so a controller that tracks the gentler kinematic ramp keeps pushing forward into the spring until the stroke completes. If the safety response instead cuts motor torque completely, the arm's effective mass <code>[LatchCompliance.m_eff_str]</code> coasts into the spring, and energy conservation sets the peak force. A spring compressed to force $\\SpringCompressionForce$ stores potential energy $\\SpringCompressionForce^2/(2\\EquivalentSeriesStiffness)$; at the tripwire, the spring already holds $\\ContactTripwireForce^2/(2\\EquivalentSeriesStiffness)$, and the coasting mass adds its kinetic energy $\\EffectiveImpactMass{}\\SpeedAtDetection^2/2$. The resulting peak impact force is $\\sqrt{\\ContactTripwireForce^2 + \\EquivalentSeriesStiffness\\,\\EffectiveImpactMass\\,\\SpeedAtDetection^2}$, the dynamic impact regime that governs high-speed robot collisions [@haddadin2009requirements]. With the series spring installed, this coasting peak is <code>[LatchCompliance.f_coast_compliant_fast_str]</code>, below the <code>[LatchCompliance.f_floor_str]</code> that the braked case reaches. That result is not a paradox. The stiffness bound was sized for a drive that holds the arm to the gentle <code>[LatchCompliance.a_arm_str]</code> stop and so keeps pushing through the whole <code>[LatchCompliance.stroke_fast_str]</code> stroke, bringing the force right to the floor. With torque removed, nothing pushes; the spring alone slows the <code>[LatchCompliance.m_eff_str]</code> effective mass, harder than that commanded stop, and halts it in a shorter stroke at a lower force. Striking the stiff plate without compliance, by contrast, produces peak forces of <code>[LatchCompliance.f_coast_rigid_fast_str]</code> at the faster approach speed and <code>[LatchCompliance.f_coast_rigid_guarded_str]</code> under the guarded approach. Within the qualified approach-speed and stroke envelope, the compliant element keeps both modeled responses at or below the latch's declared design strength; driving beyond that stroke would bottom out the spring and break the bound. Even so, passive compliance does not verify latch strength: qualification must still test damping, material fatigue, and structural load paths rather than spring stiffness alone.</p>\n<p>The door latch, the oil film, and the packaging tab pose the same problem. When a safety premise cannot be evaluated before an execution deadline, the first question is whether an admissible probe can separate the states in time. If it can, active probing supplies the missing observation; otherwise proof testing, a conservative action, passive containment, or a restricted operational domain must support the claim. The choice among them depends on the claim each response can support and the evidence needed to maintain it, and every such mitigation must also state the evidence that would retire it.</p>\n<p>The warehouse machine's hardest limits are premises its own telemetry cannot check, and each one needs an entry, a restriction, and a response chosen before the deadline.</p>\n<ul class=\"cs-list\">\n<li>[ ] <strong>Friction estimate</strong>: A friction survey measures an aisle at $\\CoulombFrictionCoefficient$ = <code>[OilFilm.mu_check_str]</code>. Given the <code>[OilFilm.a_brake_str]</code> braking bound, compute the available deceleration from @eq-frontier-credible-deceleration and determine whether the brake or the floor binds.</li>\n<li>[ ] <strong>Register entry</strong>: For the weakened door latch, state the four components of its register entry: the missing observable, the restriction, the owner, and the closure test.</li>\n<li>[ ] <strong>Shield credit</strong>: Explain why @eq-frontier-shielded-hazard gives no credit against the oil film, and identify which term the film would enter.</li>\n<li>[ ] <strong>Hidden-state responses</strong>: For a state hidden under the current observation policy, identify the five possible engineering responses, determine when active probing is available, and classify the series spring at the latch.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h2\">Choosing the Next Capability</h2>\n<p>Pouring engineering effort into a faster proposer when an unmeasured floor or an unseated tab actually limits the cell leaves the operating restriction in place. In a deployed packaging cell, restricted transit speeds and human-supervised sign-offs call for different investments. Speeding up proposal generation improves throughput inside the current safety envelope, whereas qualifying a new contact or engagement sensor can retire the need for human sign-off altogether. Each advance requires two deliverables: a predeclared task metric and a physical test of the operating premise it changes. Tuning execution inside an existing envelope and gathering the evidence to widen that envelope are distinct engineering programs, and the register's open entries decide which one the next capability needs.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-6\" class=\"cs-h3\">Match the experiment to the missing evidence</h3>\n<p>Treating every open entry in the residual-claims register by training another neural detector fails whenever the missing evidence is a field evaluation, a mathematical bound, or a fallback that the body cannot execute. The open entries do not all call for the same kind of work; each falls into one of four gap classes, and the class, not the entry's urgency, decides which experiment can close it:</p>\n<ul class=\"cs-list\">\n<li><em>Unmeasured observables</em> require dedicated sensors that can tell the relevant physical states apart before the action deadline passes.</li>\n<li><em>Evaluation deficits</em> require randomized field trials or statistical estimators that represent the actual operating population while accounting for correlated samples and rare tail events.</li>\n<li><em>Theoretical deficits</em> require bounded analytical arguments whose physical premises can be proved mathematically or tested on the actual hardware plant.</li>\n<li><em>Scope mismatches</em> require changing the system architecture or narrowing the operational design domain.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Naming the exact gap class directs the next experiment to the evidence a register entry actually lacks, turning the open premises in the claims register into distinct work programs. Undetected floor films, hidden cross-aisle pedestrians, ambiguous object identities, and unseated packaging tabs are unmeasured observables. Each calls for either a qualified sensor channel with a tested miss bound or an operating restriction that avoids the hazard, such as the downstream manual inspection that stands in for the tab sensor. Loaded dispatch-to-admission timing requires a supported timing bound under declared workload and resource limits, challenged by loaded and adversarial latency measurements. By contrast, the coverage factor $\\ShieldInterceptionCoverage$ for a safety shield requires statistical qualification on the declared population of hazardous proposals, whereas finite stress tests alone cannot establish a worst-case timing bound. Gaps between formal plant models and physical hardware are theoretical deficits, requiring robust control bounds. A scope mismatch differs from all three because no measurement, trial, or proof inside the current architecture can fix it. Claiming a de-energized safe stop for a balancing biped is the clearest example: cutting torque is itself the hazard, so the remedy is a different fallback architecture or a narrower claim, as @sec-frontier-four-open-challenges discusses.</p>\n<p>Statistical extreme value theory bounds rare tail exceedances only when its asymptotic and independence assumptions hold; fitting a parametric curve to test data cannot validate unmodeled contact physics or reveal unobserved floor friction. Similarly, Hamilton-Jacobi reachability analysis yields invariant sets only for an explicitly declared plant and disturbance bound. Moving an unverified friction assumption from field testing into a rigid-body simulator or an analytical proof leaves it just as unverified as before.</p>\n<p>↪ Extreme value theory and its asymptotic and independence assumptions are developed in @sec-appendix-ml-evt.</p>\n<p>↪ Hamilton-Jacobi reachability and its declared disturbance model are developed in @sec-appendix-control-hj-reachability.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-7\" class=\"cs-h3\">What a restriction costs</h3>\n<pre class=\"cs-code\"><code>#| echo: false\n#| warning: false\n#| message: false\n# ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n# │ THE PRICE OF A RESTRICTION\n# ├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n# │ Context: @sec-frontier-deployment-economics.\n# │ Goal:    Price the oil-film entry&#x27;s restricted mode in the one unit that\n# │          holds on every site, time per aisle leg, and set it beside the\n# │          size of the campaigns that would retire it.\n# │ Show:    the speed cut, cruise time per leg at both speeds, the added time,\n# │          and the closure and coverage campaign sizes from the cells above.\n# │ How:     cruise time = leg length / speed; the difference compares steady\n# │          cruise only and does not bound the full-cycle time difference.\n# └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nfrom mlsysim import *\nfrom mlsysim.core.units import dimensionless, meter, second\n\nAISLE = Embodied.Scenario.WarehouseAisle\n\nclass RestrictionCost:\n    # ┌── 1. LOAD ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n    _TRANSIT = Scenarios.WarehouseRestrictedTransit  # the aisle leg at the restricted speed\n    v_nominal = AISLE.v_aisle  # category: chosen (normal-mode aisle speed)\n    v_restricted = _TRANSIT.restricted_speed  # category: chosen (restricted mode on inspected floors)\n    leg = AISLE.aisle_length  # category: illustrative (aisle length)\n    totes_reported = 100000  # Agility Robotics (2025), Digit moves over 100,000 totes in commerci...  # category: measured, vendor-reported (Agility Robotics, 20 Nov 2025: Digit at GXO Flowery Branch)\n\n    # ┌── 2. EXECUTE ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n    speed_cut = _TRANSIT.speed_cut\n    t_leg_nominal = _TRANSIT.leg_time_nominal\n    t_leg_restricted = _TRANSIT.leg_time_restricted\n    t_leg_added = _TRANSIT.leg_time_added\n    time_increase = _TRANSIT.time_increase\n\n    # ┌── 3. GUARD ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n    check(abs(speed_cut - 0.2308) &lt; 5e-4, f&quot;Speed cut should be 23.1 percent, got {speed_cut}.&quot;)\n    check(abs(t_leg_nominal.magnitude - 23.077) &lt; 5e-3, f&quot;Nominal leg should take 23.1 s, got {t_leg_nominal}.&quot;)\n    check(abs(t_leg_restricted.magnitude - 30.0) &lt; 1e-9, f&quot;Restricted leg should take 30.0 s, got {t_leg_restricted}.&quot;)\n    check(abs(time_increase - 0.30) &lt; 1e-9, f&quot;Leg time should rise 30 percent, got {time_increase}.&quot;)\n    check(time_increase &gt; speed_cut, &quot;A speed cut lengthens the leg by more than its own percentage.&quot;)\n\n    # ┌── 4. OUTPUT ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n    v_nominal_str = fmt_velocity(v_nominal, precision=1)\n    v_restricted_str = fmt_velocity(v_restricted, precision=0)\n    leg_str = fmt_length(leg, unit=meter, precision=0)\n    speed_cut_str = fmt_percent(speed_cut, precision=1, style=&quot;prose&quot;)\n    t_leg_nominal_str = fmt_latency(t_leg_nominal, unit=second, precision=1)\n    t_leg_restricted_str = fmt_latency(t_leg_restricted, unit=second, precision=0)\n    time_increase_str = fmt_percent(time_increase, precision=0, style=&quot;prose&quot;)\n    totes_reported_str = fmt_int(totes_reported, commas=True)\n]```\n\nKnowing which experiment would close an entry does not say whether running it is worth the cost. An entry in the residual-claims register can buy protection at a throughput cost. On the warehouse machine, the oil-film restriction lowers cruising speed along the aisle from `[RestrictionCost.v_nominal_str]` to `[RestrictionCost.v_restricted_str]`, imposing a `[RestrictionCost.speed_cut_str]` speed penalty. At steady cruise, this derating stretches transit time across each `[RestrictionCost.leg_str]` aisle segment from `[RestrictionCost.t_leg_nominal_str]` to `[RestrictionCost.t_leg_restricted_str]`. That single constraint adds `[RestrictionCost.time_increase_str]` more transit time per leg before accounting for acceleration profiles and docking maneuvers. Because transit time scales as $1/\\SpeedAtDetection$, the percentage increase in leg time exceeds the percentage cut in speed.\n\nAn operational restriction remains in force until its closure test passes. That test carries a tangible empirical cost: the `[OilFilm.n_closure_str]` seeded-film physical trials specified in the register, for example. The separate claim about safety shield coverage requires `[ArchitecturalShieldDilution.n_coverage_str]` seeded hazardous proposals; that campaign does not close the film entry. The claims register therefore confronts systems engineers with an economic optimization problem alongside the technical one. On throughput savings alone, a formal closure campaign pays back when the cumulative value recovered exceeds the combined cost of the test campaign and the sensing instrument it qualifies. A full investment decision must also account for changes in exposure, maintenance, and the value of newly supported tasks. An operational restriction can remain the economical choice when its physical premise can be maintained reliably at low cost, such as through qualified cleaning and spill-control procedures along a few pallet-jack routes. Evaluating this trade-off requires site-specific parameters: the economic value of a completed task, the machine&#x27;s active duty hours on restricted routes, and the marginal expense of executing controlled test trials. In the absence of proprietary site economics, time provides the universal benchmark that transfers directly across facilities.\n\nThe same operational accounting governs fleet deployments. When commercial logistics facilities contract bipedal humanoids under robots-as-a-service agreements [@gxo2024agility], commercial performance is measured by volume moved, `[RestrictionCost.totes_reported_str]` totes at an initial customer facility by late 2025 [@agility2025totes]. In these operational environments, physical interventions, speed restrictions, and withheld autonomy modes can reduce completed-task throughput; their billing effect depends on the service contract. The gating criteria established during release validation translate directly into ongoing operating expenses.\n\nIntervention rates incur a double cost. Frequent human takeovers consume costly labor hours and tether fleet operations to local technician availability. Conversely, rare interventions introduce the classic ironies of automation: an operator who seldom intervenes struggles to maintain the situational awareness and manual dexterity needed when an emergency takeover occurs [@bainbridge1983ironies]. A defensible business case therefore reports proposer autonomy, whole-machine task success, and protective responses separately, as the first fallacy in @sec-frontier-fallacies-pitfalls insists, because each carries a different price.\n\n::: {.column-margin .margin-pointer}\n↩ The labor a deployment moves onto technicians, sweep crews, and remote assistants, and the uses of their data it bars, are established in @sec-responsibility-worker-burden.\n:::\n\n### Research that changes an engineering result {#sec-frontier-four-open-challenges}\n\nThe oil-film campaign can be priced because the closure test itself is already known: run `[OilFilm.n_closure_str]` paired braking trials on a representative spill or qualify an online probe. Other open entries in a register cannot be closed by scheduling a standard test campaign because no engineering method yet supplies the missing evidence. In physical AI, research warrants industry and academic investment when its resolution improves useful task performance within justified limits or supplies support for a broader operating claim. Six central challenges define this research frontier as of 2026 (@tbl-18-frontier-agenda); each stresses one of the four laws, falls into one gap class of @sec-frontier-four-gap-classes, and names the form of evidence that would count as progress.\n\nGeneralist and cross-embodiment policies stress the fourth law, permission must be earned, because evidence gathered on one body and one model version must now earn permission for another. A single pretrained vision-language-action (VLA) foundation model can now control diverse kinematics. For example, flow-matching architectures have been trained across single-arm, dual-arm, and mobile manipulation platforms from a unified model [@black2024pi0], using datasets from different embodiments. Modern pipelines continuously fine-tune from production deployments via reinforcement learning with human interventions [@physicalintelligence2025recap] or remap learned trajectories across distinct kinematic morphologies [@geminirobotics2025gemini15].\n\n::: {.column-margin .margin-pointer}\n↩ Why demonstrations logged on one embodiment carry its dynamics and do not transfer unchanged to another is established in @sec-data-cross-embodiment.\n:::\n\nHowever, empirical safety evidence compiled on a dual-arm cobot cannot be assumed to hold when that same policy executes on a mobile manipulator. Policies that continually adapt in production also require reopening the release claims whose supporting evidence depends on the changed behavior (@sec-release-deployment-lifecycle). The enduring challenge is establishing formal transfer guarantees across both physical embodiments and continuous model updates. Concrete progress requires rigorous transfer arguments with explicit kinematic and dynamic equivalence conditions, verifiable by predicting a new morphology&#x27;s closed-loop failure distributions before physical deployment. The cross-embodiment approach also reinforces the necessity of an independent permission pipeline: physical protection limits are calibrated to each specific mechanical chassis and remain invariant regardless of how frequently upstream generalist weights are updated.\n\nWhereas cross-embodiment transfer tests the fourth law, data scaling is the empirical frontier of the second law, endogenous data. Large-scale empirical studies encompassing more than 40,000 real-robot teleoperated demonstrations indicate that out-of-distribution generalization across novel environments and visual scenes follows an approximate power law governed by environmental diversity, whereas collecting redundant demonstrations within identical scenes shows sharp diminishing returns [@lin2025datascaling]. In physical systems, structural diversity rather than raw sample count dictates data utility.\n\nThe open challenge is determining whether these empirical scaling laws generalize to contact-rich manipulation, long-horizon multi-step planning, and safety-critical edge states. In embodied settings, an agent&#x27;s closed-loop control actions actively determine its subsequent perceptual distributions, concentrating risk in rare, unmodeled corner cases. Meaningful progress requires publishing verified scaling curves that measure tail-risk closed-loop failure rates within declared operational design domains (ODDs), replacing benchmark evaluations that measure only average-case task completion rates.\n\n::: {.column-margin .margin-pointer}\n↩ Why the diversity of pooled demonstrations, not their raw count, governs their value to a target task is established in @sec-data-scaling-frontier.\n:::\n\nGenerative world models also stress the fourth law, because they offer a way around the physical exposure wall only if their rollouts count as evidence. Latent dynamics formulations and high-fidelity video generation models increasingly act as general-purpose physics simulators [@nvidia2025cosmos; @assran2025vjepa2]. Researchers have begun deploying these generative models directly as policy evaluators, demonstrating strong correlation with real-robot task execution on benchmark tasks [@jeon2026roboworld]. Synthetic rollouts execute at large parallel scale for a fraction of the cost of physical hardware hours.\n\nNevertheless, strict evidentiary limits constrain synthetic evaluation. A learned world model may estimate latent physical variables, but its training fit does not validate those estimates in unobserved regimes or justify extrapolating friction discontinuities or structural compliance omitted from its training distribution. The central research question is establishing when synthetic evaluation in a learned world model constitutes valid evidentiary support for real-world release claims. Genuine progress requires formalizing proxy-transfer bounds (@sec-evaluation-proxy-transfer-law) validated against the empirical tail of real-world physical failures rather than aggregate nominal success, accompanied by end-to-end dataset and architecture provenance tracking.\n\n::: {.column-margin .margin-pointer}\n↩ Provenance records for learned world models, binding architecture, checkpoint, and dataset lineage, are established in @sec-training-world-models.\n:::\n\nDynamically stable machines, such as bipedal humanoids and high-speed quadrupeds, are the most stringent test of the first law, irreversibility. As highlighted in perspective \\ref{capstone-nervous-humanoid-crucible}, perspective \\ref{capstone-evaluation-humanoid-crucible}, perspective \\ref{capstone-placement-humanoid-crucible}, and perspective \\ref{capstone-release-humanoid-crucible}, the entire architectural chain of proposal, permission, and admission applies directly to balancing robots, with one catastrophic exception: fallback safe states. For the wheeled platform or stationary manipulator considered here, the fallback first reaches a qualified, mechanically restrained standstill before removing drive torque. On a balancing biped in mid-stride, cutting motor power induces an unrecoverable ballistic fall, transforming the safety mechanism into the primary hazard. Instead of bringing the system to an immediate stop, a balancing machine must execute dynamic recovery maneuvers: taking a stabilizing step, initiating a controlled crouch, or executing an active powered descent to a mechanically supported resting posture. Traditional functional safety standards, however, were built around fail-passive de-energization (the torque-removal rungs of the fallback ladder, which @sec-enforcement-four-rungs treats as fail-safe only for a plant already at a mechanically restrained standstill). This created what safety researchers term the *fail-passive gap* [@ding2026failpassive]. Bridging this divide requires certifying active closed-loop balance stabilization as a safety-rated critical function. Demonstrating meaningful progress requires designing certifiable dynamic reaction chains, analogous to automotive emergency braking profiles. These chains must reliably steer the robot from each qualified gait phase, under the declared fault conditions, into a stable, supported configuration within bounded peak power and kinetic energy budgets. The most capable learned machines are thus the ones for which a safe stop is hardest to define.\n\n: **Fail-Passive and Fail-Operational**: Avionics separates a fail-passive function, whose failure causes no significant deviation of path or attitude and hands the task back to the crew, from a fail-operational one, which keeps performing through a failure. Removing torque from a wheeled base is fail-passive in this sense only after it reaches a qualified, mechanically restrained rest. A balancing biped in mid-stride has no such resting state, so its posture control must remain operational until the machine reaches a supported rest, and the certifiable reaction must be a controlled one.\n\nMoving from whole-body balance to the end-effector, contact-rich, dexterous manipulation centers on physical AI&#x27;s canonical unobserved state: high-frequency contact interaction. Determining whether a snap-fit latch yields, a packaging tab seats, or a fragile container begins to crush cannot be reliably deduced from joint motor currents or external RGB-D cameras alone. High-resolution tactile sensing provides a direct channel for several of these contact dynamics; each claimed observation still needs qualification for its materials, geometry, and deadline. Modern vision-based elastomeric tactile sensors directly capture surface deformation fields, contact geometry, and distributed shear stresses at the end-effector [@yuan2017gelsight].\n\n::: {.column-margin .margin-pointer}\n↩ Optical elastomer tactile sensing and its timing and calibration obligations are established in @sec-perception-tactile-grounding.\n:::\n\nClosing the loop on contact, however, challenges the third law, proposal is not permission. Contact forces escalate within milliseconds of initial impact, far outstripping the round-trip latency of high-level neural planners and placing strict burdens on low-level passive or series elastic compliance (@sec-frontier-passive-compliance-containment). The open engineering challenge is integrating tactile feedback into the formal permission path. Concrete progress requires qualifying tactile perception channels with rigorous statistical miss rates for incipient slip and shear yield, coupled with low-latency impedance controllers whose force limits can be deterministically verified and bounded by safety hardware [@tedrake2023manipulation].\n\nCertifying learned models that directly implement safety functions is the central challenge of the fourth law. Although such components are assigned the notified-body route of @sec-release-standards-map, the robotics industry lacks standardized methodologies to certify deep neural networks at automotive or industrial safety integrity levels (SIL) or PL, because current verification techniques face stark limitations. Exact satisfiability modulo theories (SMT)-based and mixed-integer linear programming verification tools can establish formal input-output properties over declared neural network weights [@katz2017reluplex], yet these proofs remain vulnerable to open-world modeling gaps outside the declared plant and observations. Purely statistical validation collides with the exposure wall, requiring billions of physical test cycles to establish low target failure probabilities. Runtime safety filters and shield wrappers transfer the verification burden onto the shield&#x27;s own coverage and latency envelope [@hsu2024safetyfilter]. The unresolved systems challenge is constructing hybrid safety arguments: composing analytical proofs where deterministic physics models are tractable with statistical bounds where perceptual models operate, and allocating risk budgets across both tiers. Until such composite certification arguments mature, the architecture developed here keeps learned proposers outside the safety-critical permission path. Verifiable progress requires publishing complete, audited conformity dossiers for learned safety components alongside their formal assurance cases.\n\n\\Needspace{24\\baselineskip}\n\n| Open problem                              | Law stressed               | Gap class             | Progress would be                                                                      | State of practice, as of 2026                                                                                                                                                                        |\n|:------------------------------------------|:---------------------------|:----------------------|:---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|:-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| **Generalist, cross-embodiment policies** | Permission must be earned  | Evaluation deficit    | Evidence that transfers across bodies and updates under a stated equivalence condition | One policy across several robot types [@black2024pi0]; learning from deployment with corrections [@physicalintelligence2025recap]; motion transfer between embodiments [@geminirobotics2025gemini15] |\n| **Data scaling**                          | Endogenous data            | Evaluation deficit    | Scaling curves on closed-loop failure rates inside a declared ODD                      | Roughly power-law gains in environment and object diversity, saturating in demonstrations per environment [@lin2025datascaling]                                                                      |\n| **World models as evaluators**            | Permission must be earned  | Evaluation deficit    | A proxy-transfer condition validated on the failure tail                               | General world foundation models [@nvidia2025cosmos; @assran2025vjepa2]; a world model built for policy evaluation [@jeon2026roboworld]                                                               |\n| **Fall-safe dynamically stable machines** | Irreversibility            | Scope mismatch        | A certified, actively controlled reaction chain to a supported rest state              | The fail-passive gap located in a humanoid pick-and-place cell [@ding2026failpassive]                                                                                                                |\n| **Contact-rich, dexterous manipulation**  | Proposal is not permission | Unmeasured observable | A qualified tactile channel and a checkable contact-force bound                        | High-resolution vision-based tactile sensing [@yuan2017gelsight]                                                                                                                                     |\n| **Certifying learned safety functions**   | Permission must be earned  | Theoretical deficit   | A published conformity assessment of a learned safety component                        | Third-party assessment required in the EU from 20 January 2027 [@eu2023machinery]; network verification of declared models [@katz2017reluplex]                                                       |\n\n: **A research agenda keyed to the four laws, as of 2026**: Each row names an open problem, the law it stresses, the gap class of @sec-frontier-four-gap-classes it belongs to, and the evidence that would count as progress. The last column dates the state of practice and should be refreshed in later editions; the durable content is in the first four columns. {#tbl-18-frontier-agenda tbl-colwidths=&quot;[18,15,14,25,28]&quot;}\n\nTwo systems challenges cut across all six problems. The first is continual online adaptation, which turns the model into a moving target. Whether a proposer retrains asynchronously on aggregated fleet telemetry or adapts its latent state online to accommodate component wear and shifting terrain [@kumar2021rma], background model updates must never starve the permission pipeline of compute or bus bandwidth. The second is multi-robot fleet coordination, which couples otherwise independent permission boundaries. Transmitting shared task intents across lossy wireless links cannot substitute for the local, hard-real-time sensor streams required by an individual robot&#x27;s admission gate. Physically cooperative manipulation of a shared payload likewise couples actuator contact dynamics directly across platforms. Across all of these problems, advancing physical AI does not require abandoning established systems rigor; it requires extending it. Unlocking the next capability begins by isolating a concrete physical task that the current system cannot safely execute, identifying the missing empirical premise or unmeasured observable, and engineering the precise evidential argument required to justify expanding the machine&#x27;s operational envelope.\n\n## Fallacies and Pitfalls {#sec-frontier-fallacies-pitfalls}\n\nWhen engineers move physical AI systems such as the warehouse mobile manipulator or the packaging cell from isolated benchmarks to production floors, the traps that remain are conceptual. Every local calculation can succeed numerically while the system-level reasoning fails, because the team has misread how physical dynamics, learned proposers, and runtime enforcement relate. The following traps recur on both machines.\n\n::: {.fallacy-pitfall}\n**Fallacy**: *A protective intervention means the whole machine failed its task.*\n\nWhen a runtime safety governor or control barrier filter modifies an actuator command, engineers often mistakenly log the episode as a system-level failure. In practice, a least-change speed correction or torque clamp often allows an autonomous mobile robot or packaging arm to complete its physical mission smoothly within safe boundaries. If the warehouse machine enters a route whose floor is inspected only to $\\CoulombFrictionCoefficient \\ge$ `[OilFilm.mu_insp_str]` and the governor trims its speed from the `[OilFilm.v_aisle_str]` aisle speed to the register&#x27;s `[OilFilm.v_restricted_str]` restricted mode for that floor, the delivery still completes. The leg takes `[RestrictionCost.time_increase_str]` longer.\n\nTreating every safety intervention as a mission failure corrupts operational telemetry. Conflating proposer interventions with unrecoverable task aborts conceals where engineering effort should be directed. Development teams must track three metrics independently: raw proposer autonomy (the fraction of time the learned model operates without override), whole-machine task success (whether the physical objective was achieved within operational deadlines), and protective intervention frequency. Decoupling these signals reveals whether system bottlenecks stem from policy distribution shift, sensor latency in the perception pipeline, or overly conservative governor tuning.\n:::\n\n::: {.column-margin .margin-pointer}\n↩ Proposer autonomy, task success, and protective intervention as separately recorded outcome channels are established in @sec-evaluation-what-measured.\n:::\n\n::: {.fallacy-pitfall}\n**Pitfall**: *Treating a restriction as evidence that its physical premise holds.*\n\nDeriving a tighter operational limit does not guarantee that the physical environment satisfies the assumptions behind that limit. The register&#x27;s restricted mode for the warehouse machine, `[OilFilm.v_restricted_str]` instead of `[OilFilm.v_aisle_str]`, provides a stopping margin only on floors that hold $\\CoulombFrictionCoefficient \\ge$ `[OilFilm.mu_insp_str]`. Under the clear film, where $\\CoulombFrictionCoefficient$ falls to `[OilFilm.mu_oil_str]` and the available deceleration to `[OilFilm.a_oil_str]`, the lower speed setpoint carries no stopping guarantee at all; the restriction rests on the same unmeasured premise as the mode it replaced.\n\nDigital artifacts cannot substitute for physical friction. Neither a signed configuration parameter in software, nor a clear path reported by an RGB camera, proves that the ground provides adequate traction. Vision pipelines readily mistake transparent oil or thin ice for dry concrete. A restricted envelope therefore needs its own premise kept true, through the inspection and spill procedure the register assigns to facility operations or through a qualified forward film detector. Monitoring wheel slip and inertial deceleration can reveal, after the fact, that a premise failed, and should trigger the restriction for later motion, but it cannot rescue the stop already under way. A safety envelope is only as credible as the physical premise beneath it.\n:::\n\n::: {.fallacy-pitfall}\n**Fallacy**: *More model capacity or simulated exposure settles every residual claim.*\n\nScaling up neural network parameter counts or running billions of simulated training steps improves policy interpolation across modeled conditions, but neither technique can overcome an unmodeled physical reality. A deep learning policy cannot infer an unobserved physical state, such as internal material stress, micro-slip at a fingertip, or variable ground compliance, if the sensor suite lacks the transducers to measure it. Similarly, physics simulators rely on simplified approximations of rigid-body contact, Coulomb friction, and non-deformable geometry. Generating large synthetic datasets within a simulator that omits real contact mechanics merely produces policies optimized for fictitious physical dynamics.\n\nWhen an embodied system displays persistent edge-case failures, the solution rarely lies in adding more transformer layers or randomizing synthetic textures. Engineers must diagnose the specific physical claim that lacks empirical grounding. Resolving the defect requires choosing the appropriate engineering intervention: integrating a dedicated physical sensor to observe the missing state, introducing passive mechanical compliance to absorb unmodeled impact energy, constraining the operational design domain, or calibrating friction models against instrumented hardware trials.\n:::\n\n::: {.fallacy-pitfall}\n**Pitfall**: *Charging the full stop time against an action deadline.*\n\nAn engineer who sizes the latest useful observation by subtracting the whole braking duration treats the machine as if it held its speed until it stopped instantly. For the jerk-limited stop from the `[OilFilm.v_aisle_str]` aisle speed, braking lasts `[OilFilm.t_brake_aisle_str]` but covers only the distance of `[OilFilm.t_brake_equiv_str]` of travel at constant speed. The time form therefore over-reserves `[OilFilm.t_overreserve_str]`, or `[OilFilm.d_overreserve_str]`, more than the entire `[OilFilm.spare_aisle_str]` spare.\n\nThe error is safe in the narrow sense that it never admits an unsafe stop, which is why it survives review. Its cost appears elsewhere. A deadline computed this way refuses operating modes the distance form correctly admits, and it hides how much timing allowance a new detector or a slower admission stage can still spend. The deadline belongs to the remaining distance, @eq-frontier-action-deadline, with the braking term divided by speed, never to the stop&#x27;s duration.\n:::\n\n::: {.fallacy-pitfall}\n**Fallacy**: *A safety shield makes the proposer&#x27;s error rate irrelevant.*\n\nA shield attenuates hazardous proposals; it does not erase them. In notebook \\ref{nbk-frontier-fleet-exposure-and-shielded-decomposition}, a full release cycle of adjudicated proposals with no hazardous event still bounds the proposal rate only at `[ArchitecturalShieldDilution.p_proposal_upper_sci_str]` $\\text{h}^{-1}$. A shield with interception coverage `[ArchitecturalShieldDilution.c_shield_str]` then leaves a modeled residual of `[ArchitecturalShieldDilution.p_system_conditional_sci_str]` $\\text{h}^{-1}$, above the `[ArchitecturalShieldDilution.p_target_sci_str]` $\\text{h}^{-1}$ target even with the common-cause term set to zero. Meeting the target at that proposal bound would need coverage of `[ArchitecturalShieldDilution.c_required_str]`, and showing the assumed coverage alone takes `[ArchitecturalShieldDilution.n_coverage_str]` seeded hazardous proposals with no miss.\n\nThe residual of @eq-frontier-shielded-hazard is a product, so both factors need evidence, and a common-cause failure that blinds the shield in the same states where the proposer errs lowers coverage exactly where it matters. The shield is the reason the proposer need not be perfect, not a reason to stop measuring how often it errs.\n:::\n\n::: {.fallacy-pitfall}\n**Pitfall**: *Qualifying a forward detector by its miss rate alone.*\n\nA floor-film detector that passes `[OilFilm.n_closure_str]` failure-free seeded trials has a miss bound of `[OilFilm.p_miss_target_pct_str]`, and a team may treat that result as closing the register entry. Detection that arrives too late is a miss in every sense that matters to the stop. At the `[OilFilm.v_aisle_str]` aisle speed with a `[OilFilm.t_detect_str]` sensing and inference latency, @eq-frontier-film-detection-range requires the detector to see the film `[OilFilm.d_detect_min_str]` ahead, about the whole `[OilFilm.d_clear_str]` clear distance. At that range the latency leaves nothing for the `[OilFilm.delta_da_str]` dispatch-to-admission allocation. To fit beside it, the detector&#x27;s latency must stay under the `[OilFilm.reserve_da_str]` reserve.\n\nA detector that reports only once the wheels are on the film faces a `[OilFilm.stop_oil_str]` stop, turning a statistically accurate classification into an unrecoverable overrun. The closure test must therefore predeclare range, latency, false-alarm rate, and the friction assumed after detection, alongside the miss rate, and qualify them together on the routes the restriction covers.\n:::\n\n## Summary {#sec-frontier-summary}\n\nThe warehouse mobile manipulator now carries one connected design argument, and the packaging cell shows that the same argument transfers to a machine with different mechanics, rates, and hazards. The book opened by asking why a better prediction can still produce a worse physical action, and the chapter&#x27;s answer is concrete. A neural network optimizes expected statistical loss over its training distribution, but the physical plant operates under continuous Newtonian mechanics, electromagnetism, and thermodynamics. A better proposer cannot supply the friction premise that an unobserved oil film removes, because no input it receives measures the tire-ground contact interface. It cannot make a derated operating mode evaluated, because the operational restriction that shortens the stop also displaces the proposer into physical configurations its training and evaluation never sampled. It cannot close a detectorless claim such as the glare-hidden packaging tab, because the physical sensor required to resolve that state does not exist on the machine. Nor can a faster forward pass recover the stopping distance already consumed while the vehicle cruised under unguided momentum ($\\BlindTravelDistance = \\SpeedAtDetection \\PrebrakeDelay$). In each case, the physical outcome is judged by dynamics, timing, and environmental evidence that the learned prediction does not contain.\n\nThe learned model must therefore be engineered as an unprivileged component of a machine, with the rest of the machine supplying what the model lacks, one law at a time. The first law, irreversibility, turns the body&#x27;s kinetic, actuator, and thermal limits into budgets that bound any proposal, and the friction floor showed that a single unmeasured term can void those budgets. The second law, endogenous data, confines competence to the states the closed loop actually visits, which is why the packaging cell&#x27;s restricted mode opened a regime its evaluation never sampled. The third law, proposal is not permission, places an independent permission path between proposal and actuator, and the shield-dilution bound showed that this path improves a hazard-rate claim only for the hazards it can detect and intercept in time. The fourth law, permission must be earned, grants the machine only what its evidence supports, and the residual-claims register gives each unobserved premise a restriction, an owner, and a predeclared closure test. @Tbl-18-key-equations collects the relations the chapter applied to both machines, what each bounds or decides, and where the book derives it.\n\n\\Needspace{16\\baselineskip}\n\n| Equation                                                                                                                                                                       | What it bounds or decides                                                                            | Where derived                                                      |\n|:-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|:-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|:-------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| $\\StoppingDistance = \\SpeedAtDetection\\PrebrakeDelay + \\SpeedAtDetection^2/2\\CredibleBrakingDeceleration$                                                                      | Whether a mode&#x27;s stop fits its clearance, and whether delay or braking dominates                     | @eq-body-stopping-distance                                         |\n| $\\SpeedBound = -\\CredibleBrakingDeceleration\\PrebrakeDelay + \\sqrt{(\\CredibleBrakingDeceleration\\PrebrakeDelay)^2 + 2\\CredibleBrakingDeceleration{}\\AvailableBrakingDistance}$ | The speed ceiling a stopping budget admits                                                           | @eq-body-velocity-ceiling; jerk ramps in @eq-enforce-speed-ceiling |\n| $\\AvailableDeceleration = \\min(\\CredibleBrakingDeceleration, \\CoulombFrictionCoefficient \\Gravity)$                                                                            | Whether the brake or the floor binds, and the friction floor $\\CredibleBrakingDeceleration/\\Gravity$ | @eq-frontier-credible-deceleration                                 |\n| $\\SystemHazardRate \\le \\HazardousProposalRate(1-\\ShieldInterceptionCoverage) + \\HardwareCommonCauseRate$                                                                       | What a shield adds to a hazard-rate claim, and the coverage the claim needs                          | @eq-frontier-shielded-hazard                                       |\n| $\\ZeroFailureTrialCount = \\lceil \\ln\\HypothesisTestSignificanceLevel / \\ln(1-\\TrialFailureProbability) \\rceil$                                                                 | Failure-free trials that bound a miss or false-accept probability $\\TrialFailureProbability$         | @eq-eval-zero-failure-sample-size                                  |\n| $\\RequiredDetectionRange \\ge \\SpeedAtDetection\\,\\DetectionInterval + \\KnownComponentStopDistance(\\SpeedAtDetection)$                                                           | The range at which a detector must report a hazard ahead of the machine                              | @eq-frontier-film-detection-range                                  |\n| $\\RemainingHarmDistance \\ge \\SpeedAtDetection\\,(\\EnforcerComputeTime + \\ActuatorResponseTime) + \\BrakingDistance(\\SpeedAtDetection)$                                           | The latest point at which a distinguishing observation can still help                                | @eq-frontier-action-deadline                                       |\n| $\\ContactTripwireForce + \\EquivalentSeriesStiffness\\,\\PostContactDrivenStroke \\le \\LatchStrengthFloor$                                                                         | The stiffness of a passive element that bounds contact force                                         | @eq-frontier-passive-compliance                                    |\n\n: **Key equations of the chapter**: Each equation the chapter applies to the warehouse machine or the packaging cell, what it bounds or decides, and where the book derives it. The first two and the zero-failure count are derived in earlier chapters and reused here; the others are stated in this chapter. {#tbl-18-key-equations tbl-colwidths=&quot;[42,36,22]&quot;}\n\n::: {.callout-takeaways title=&quot;Engineering a machine that senses and acts&quot;}\n\n* **Useful service joins the four laws:** Body limits, learned competence, independent permission, and maintained evidence must support the same task; a better prediction strengthens only one link of that argument.\n* **Competence can grow:** New qualified experience, retraining, and regression evaluation can extend what the next candidate can justify.\n* **Admission can preserve success:** A minimally corrected proposal can complete useful work; proposer autonomy, task success, and protection require separate measures.\n* **Restrictions need evidence:** A lower speed still needs traction, clearance, and a feasible response. A stipulated supported branch teaches the release decision without turning illustrative records into measurements.\n* **Residual limits select the next experiment:** Missing observations, evaluation support, proof premises, and task scope call for different engineering responses.\n:::\n\nThe chain from body limits to a release decision can be carried through for any machine, but the chapter&#x27;s last lesson lies where that chain runs out. The running machine&#x27;s hardest limits were not its slowest computation or its weakest model; they were premises no sensor reports, a floor&#x27;s friction and a latch&#x27;s strength. Engineering judgment at that edge means naming such a premise, restricting the machine until evidence arrives, and choosing the next measurement, learning experiment, architectural change, or narrower domain by which open premise it retires. @Sec-boundary promised the ability to design, teach, integrate, evaluate, and maintain such a machine within justified operating limits, and to name, when the evidence is incomplete, what would support the next capability. That ability to build, verify, and justify a machine that senses and acts is the enduring discipline this book develops.\n\n::: {.callout-chapter-connection title=&quot;From physical AI to the systems discipline&quot;}\nThe next unfamiliar machine will bring a different body, task, and environment. Its engineering begins by tracing how observations become proposals and how admitted commands become force, motion, and heat. The methods developed here provide a way to teach useful behavior, integrate it with timely physical control, and determine which operating claims the resulting evidence supports. This work is dedicated to the students, researchers, and systems engineers who take up the challenge of giving machines safe and capable hands in an open physical world.\n:::\n</code></pre>\n<p>\\vspace{5mm} \\begingroup \\raggedleft</p>\n<pre class=\"cs-code\"><code>\n*Prof. Vijay Janapa Reddi, Harvard University*\n</code></pre>\n<p>\\vspace{10mm} \\endgroup</p>\n<pre class=\"cs-code\"><code></code></pre>\n<p>\\part{key:backmatter}</p>", "quizzes": []};
